// MODULES ARE ACQUIRED BY DEMAND, ONE EXACT READ PER REQUESTED MODULE (source layout authority R9).
// The compiler starts from an entry module, parses it, takes every module its imports and qualified
// references name, resolves each name to a file through the storage layout, reads that file, and
// repeats. The closure is whatever was requested: no directory is walked, no file is read that a
// request did not name, and a file that is malformed but not requested changes neither the verdict
// nor the read set. Whole-population jobs (the layout check, the population parse) are explicit
// enumerations of the layout; they are never a side effect of compiling one entry.
//
// WHAT IS REQUESTABLE. An import names a module exactly. A qualified reference of two or more
// spine segments (the QualifiedName list, not a string split on `.`) names a module and a member,
// split by the storage lookup's longest-prefix probe. A String literal is never a module reference:
// its payload is one interned atom even when the lexeme contains dots, so identity strings and
// DeclarationRef.module_path data do not drive acquisition. A BARE NAME IS NOT REQUESTABLE (R1):
// nothing here derives a module from one, so a bare name that no import
// or local declaration binds reaches the resolver unbound and refuses there, naming the spelling.
// A qualified spelling that names no module requests nothing -- it may be a member access on a
// local, which the resolver judges -- while an import or entry that names no module refuses at once.
//
// A SPELLING WHOSE HEAD A VISIBLE BINDER BINDS IS A PROJECTION, NOT A MODULE REFERENCE, AND IS NEVER PROBED.
// `x.y` with `x` a parameter, a `let` binder or a module-level value is a field projection off `x`; reading
// `<root>/x/y.dag` for it would make the verdict depend on a file the program does not mention, so an
// unrelated or malformed same-named module could change what a program means. Whether a head is bound is
// decided from the lexical frames at the site (v2.compiler.lexical_scope), the producer resolution reads
// too, never from the spelling's shape. THERE ARE THREE ANSWERS, NOT TWO. A head a decided binder binds (an
// Arrow parameter, a `let` binder, a loop carrier, a module-level value) is a projection. A head nothing
// binds is a candidate. A head a match arm's pattern atom or an import MAY bind is undecided: whether the atom
// binds or names a payload constructor, and whether the import names a value, needs the resolution namespace,
// which does not exist until this module has acquired what a program names. An undecided head requests
// nothing, because the cost of guessing is not symmetric: probing for a name resolution binds as a value
// reads a file the program never mentions and, for a requester whose tree may not import the probed one, denies
// a valid program. What not requesting costs is a refusal, not a wrong answer: a spelling that did need its
// module and got none is unbound at resolution and refuses there, naming the spelling.
//
// THE DECISIONS ARE v2.compiler.source_authority's, THE READS ARE THIS MODULE'S. That module owns the
// pure decisions (which file a name is stored at, the prefix order, the tri-state probe, the
// cross-tree direction, the `module` line agreement); this module drives them through the filesystem
// handler it is bound to, so the read set is exactly the probes the decisions asked for. A probe that
// finds the file absent is not a read of any module and falls back to a shorter prefix; a probe that
// finds the file unreadable refuses the request and never falls back (DESIGN section 5). Under the
// storage rule every module file is `<name>.dag` and every directory is a bare segment, so a probe
// never walks THROUGH a file: a not-a-directory answer is a real anomaly of the tree and refuses
// with the host's kind named, never an absence.
//
// ONE FRONT-END PASS PER MEMBER FOR ITS REQUESTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT THE LAST. A qualified reference is
// only visible once the module is lowered (the retired parse-tree walker missed postfix call
// sites), so each acquired member is tokenized, parsed and normalized here to read its requests with
// an isolated occurrence allocator. The emission phase then parses the closure again with the
// ingest-wide allocator that keeps projected occurrence identities from colliding across files
// (v2.compiler.self_host.closure_emission ClosureIngest). That second pass is inherent -- the final
// allocator's seed is the end of the last file's ids, which is unknown until every member is read --
// and the work is bounded by the closure, where the retired route paid it for the whole tree.

type SourceRootBinding {
  tree: SourceRootRef
  host_directory: String
}

type AcquisitionKind
  = AcquireEntry
  | AcquireImportedModule
  | AcquireImportedMember
  | AcquireQualifiedReference

type AcquisitionRequest {
  from_module: String
  from_tree: SourceRootRef
  spelling: String
  kind: AcquisitionKind
}

// `test_markers` is the declaration names carrying the authored `test` marker, read from the member's
// own normalized tree (v2.compiler.normalize test_markers). Discovery receives them as supplied values.
type AcquiredModule {
  name: String
  file: ModuleFileRef
  host_directory: String
  content: String
  requests: List<AcquisitionRequest>
  test_markers: List<String>
  origin: AcquiredOrigin
}

// WHERE A MEMBER'S BYTES CAME FROM. A member read from its file is the ordinary case. A derived
// roster (v2.compiler.source_authority derived_roster_module) has NO file: its bytes are rendered
// from its sibling modules, and `inputs` is the key of every sibling file the derivation depended on,
// in sorted stem order. That is what lets the exactness verdict (R9) hold for it: a derivation input
// is a read the closure owes, and the roster's own storage key is never read -- a file there refuses.
type AcquiredOrigin
  = AcquiredFromFile
  | AcquiredDerivedRoster { inputs: List<String> }

// Why a derived roster could not be contributed. Every arm is located on the roster module it was
// requested as; none of them narrows the roster to the siblings that could be read.
type DerivedRosterRefusal
  = DerivedRosterOccupied { file: ModuleFileRef }
  | DerivedRosterProbeRefused { file: ModuleFileRef, cause: String }
  | DerivedRosterUnlisted { directory: String, cause: String }
  | DerivedRosterSiblingUnread { file: ModuleFileRef, cause: String }
  | DerivedRosterSiblingMisfiled { file: ModuleFileRef, held: ModuleFileRef }
  | DerivedRosterMisplacedRow { stem: String, declared: String }

type AcquisitionRefusal
  = AcquisitionGrammarUnprepared { diagnostics: NonEmptyDiagnostics }
  | AcquisitionEntryUnderivable { entry: String, cause: String }
  | AcquisitionTreeUnbound { tree: String, request: AcquisitionRequest }
  | AcquisitionUnknownModule { request: AcquisitionRequest }
  | AcquisitionLookupRefused { request: AcquisitionRequest, lookup: ReferenceLookup }
  | AcquisitionMemberRefused { module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, diagnostics: NonEmptyDiagnostics }
  | AcquisitionMemberSitesUnreadable { module: String, file: ModuleFileRef }
  | AcquisitionMemberIdentityDisagrees { module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, declared: String }
  | AcquisitionDerivedRosterRefused { module: String, refusal: DerivedRosterRefusal }

// The receipt: `reads` is the key of every file whose content was read, in the order read. Probes
// that found a file absent are not in it -- they read no module -- and are carried as `absent_probes`
// so the lookup's work is countable beside its reads.
type Acquisition
  = AcquisitionComplete { entries: List<String>, modules: List<AcquiredModule>, reads: List<String>, absent_probes: Int }
  | AcquisitionRefused { refusal: AcquisitionRefusal, reads: List<String> }

type AcquisitionEnv {
  layout: SourceStorageLayout
  bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>
  lex: PreparedLexRules
  grammar: PreparedGrammar
  header_tail: Reach
  depth: AcquisitionDepth
}

// WHAT A CLOSURE MEMBER IS ASKED TO REQUEST. The compiler's closure is the full front end of every member (normalize,
// reference sites, imported members, qualified references). A consumer whose question is only the IMPORT graph
// (is there an import path from A to B; the import graph's one structural law is acyclicity, DESIGN section 3) needs the
// parse and the module's import rows: nothing normalized, no reference site collected.
type AcquisitionDepth
  = AcquireFullClosure
  | AcquireImportClosure

// The pending requests are a stack (a member's requests are pushed in front), so a push costs the
// size of the push and not of the queue. `known` is the modules already acquired and `absent` the
// file keys already probed absent. `held` is every roster sibling's file this acquisition read
// (v2.compiler.source_authority derived_roster_sibling_of), sealed as read, by file key: a roster
// derivation takes a sibling from here instead of reading it again, and a later request for a sibling a
// derivation read is answered from here, so no file is read a second time. It holds siblings only, so
// the closure's other members are not kept twice.
type AcquisitionState {
  pending: List<AcquisitionRequest>
  modules: List<AcquiredModule>
  known: Map<String, Bool>
  absent: Map<String, Bool>
  absent_probes: Int
  reads: List<String>
  held: Map<String, HeldSourceRead>
}

type CandidateVerdict
  = CandidateKnown { module: String }
  | CandidateFresh { module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, host_directory: String, content: String }
  | CandidateUnknown
  | CandidateRefusedBy { lookup: ReferenceLookup }
  | CandidateTreeUnbound { tree: SourceRootRef }
  | CandidateDerived { module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, host_directory: String, content: String, rows: List<DerivedRowRead>, inputs: List<String> }
  | CandidateDerivedRefused { refusal: AcquisitionRefusal }

// A row a roster derivation read and selected that is not yet a member: it is admitted, from these
// bytes, before the roster itself.
type DerivedRowRead {
  module: String
  file: ModuleFileRef
  content: String
}

type CandidateLookup {
  state: AcquisitionState
  verdict: CandidateVerdict
}

type AcquisitionStep
  = AcquisitionAdvanced { state: AcquisitionState }
  | AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: AcquisitionRefusal, state: AcquisitionState }

// What one member asks for, as module spellings.
type MemberRequests {
  imports: List<String>
  members: List<String>
  references: List<String>
}

type AcquisitionFrontEnd {
  parse_root: Node
  tree: NormalizedTree
}

type AcquisitionMemberRead
  = AcquisitionMemberReadOk { requests: MemberRequests, test_markers: List<String> }
  | AcquisitionMemberReadRefused { refusal: AcquisitionRefusal }

type AcquisitionSeen {
  order: List<String>
  seen: Map<String, Bool>
}

fn acquisition_host_directory(bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>, tree: SourceRootRef) -> Optional<String> {
  match list_find_first(xs: bindings, predicate: fn(b) { source_root_ref_label(tree: b.tree) == source_root_ref_label(tree: tree) }) {
    Present { value: binding } => optional_present(value: binding.host_directory)
    Absent => optional_absent()
  }
}

// One exact read of one candidate file, answered as the lookup's tri-state. A host failure other
// than not-found is a refusal of the file and never an absence.
fn acquisition_probe(host_directory: String, file: ModuleFileRef) -> ModuleFileProbe {
  held_source_probe(read: acquisition_read_source(host_directory: host_directory, file: file))
}

// A FILE'S BYTES AS THIS ACQUISITION READ THEM. The type is sole_constructor and its one construction is
// in acquisition_read_source, the read itself, so a value of it is the bytes of exactly `file` as read at
// its host directory: bytes a caller did not read cannot be handed to a roster derivation as a sibling's
// (a construction outside this module is refused, v2.compiler.infer infer_sole_constructor_diags). What
// the seal cannot say is under which key a caller files it, so the derivation checks that too
// (derived_roster_read_siblings).
type HeldSourceRead sole_constructor {
  file: ModuleFileRef
  content: String
}

type HeldSourceProbe
  = HeldSourcePresent { read: HeldSourceRead }
  | HeldSourceAbsent { file: ModuleFileRef }
  | HeldSourceRefused { file: ModuleFileRef, cause: String }

fn acquisition_read_source(host_directory: String, file: ModuleFileRef) -> HeldSourceProbe {
  match source_root_read_file(path: source_root_child_path(directory: host_directory, name: file.relative_path)) {
    FilesystemExactPathRead { path: _, content: content } => HeldSourcePresent { read: HeldSourceRead { file: file, content: content } }
    FilesystemExactPathAbsent { path: _ } => HeldSourceAbsent { file: file }
    FilesystemExactPathUnreadable { path: _, kind: kind, error: cause } =>
      HeldSourceRefused { file: file, cause: concat(filesystem_failure_kind_name(kind: kind), ": ", cause) }
    FilesystemExactPathKindUnrecognized { path: _, observed: kind, error: cause } =>
      HeldSourceRefused { file: file, cause: concat("unrecognized filesystem failure kind ", kind, ": ", cause) }
  }
}

fn held_source_probe(read: HeldSourceProbe) -> ModuleFileProbe {
  match read {
    HeldSourcePresent { read: held } => ModuleFilePresent { file: held.file, content: held.content }
    HeldSourceAbsent { file: file } => ModuleFileAbsent { file: file }
    HeldSourceRefused { file: file, cause: cause } => ModuleFileRefused { file: file, cause: cause }
  }
}

// The state holding `read` when it is a roster sibling's file; any other read is not kept.
fn acquisition_holding(state: AcquisitionState, layout: SourceStorageLayout, module: String, read: HeldSourceProbe) -> AcquisitionState {
  match read {
    HeldSourcePresent { read: held } =>
      match derived_roster_sibling_of(layout: layout, module: module) {
        Present { value: _ } => acquisition_with_held(state: state, read: held)
        Absent => state
      }
    _ => state
  }
}

// What the acquisition holds for `file`. A read is filed under its own file's key by this module, but the
// state is an ordinary value a caller can assemble, so a read filed under another file's key is refused
// where it is looked up and never consumed as that file's bytes.
type HeldReadLookup
  = HeldReadFor { read: HeldSourceRead }
  | HeldReadNone
  | HeldReadMisfiled { held: ModuleFileRef }

fn held_read_of(held: Map<String, HeldSourceRead>, file: ModuleFileRef) -> HeldReadLookup {
  match map_lookup(m: held, key: module_file_key(file: file)) {
    Absent => HeldReadNone {}
    Present { value: read } =>
      if module_file_key(file: read.file) == module_file_key(file: file) { HeldReadFor { read: read } } else { HeldReadMisfiled { held: read.file } }
  }
}

fn held_misfiled_cause(file: ModuleFileRef, held: ModuleFileRef) -> String {
  concat("the read held under ", module_file_key(file: file), " is the read of ", module_file_key(file: held), ", so it is not that file's bytes")
}

fn acquisition_with_held(state: AcquisitionState, read: HeldSourceRead) -> AcquisitionState {
  AcquisitionState {
    pending: state.pending,
    modules: state.modules,
    known: state.known,
    absent: state.absent,
    absent_probes: state.absent_probes,
    reads: state.reads,
    held: map_insert(m: state.held, key: module_file_key(file: read.file), value: read)
  }
}

fn acquisition_request_exact(request: AcquisitionRequest) -> Bool {
  match request.kind {
    AcquireEntry => true
    AcquireImportedModule => true
    AcquireImportedMember => false
    AcquireQualifiedReference => false
  }
}

// An entry or an import names its module whole; a member or a qualified reference is split by
// probing prefixes, longest first.
fn acquisition_candidates(request: AcquisitionRequest) -> List<String> {
  if acquisition_request_exact(request: request) {
    [request.spelling]
  } else {
    reference_prefixes(reference: request.spelling)
  }
}

fn acquisition_with_absent(state: AcquisitionState, key: String) -> AcquisitionState {
  AcquisitionState {
    pending: state.pending,
    modules: state.modules,
    known: state.known,
    absent: map_insert(m: state.absent, key: key, value: true),
    absent_probes: state.absent_probes + 1,
    reads: state.reads,
    held: state.held
  }
}

fn acquisition_with_read(state: AcquisitionState, key: String) -> AcquisitionState {
  AcquisitionState {
    pending: state.pending,
    modules: state.modules,
    known: state.known,
    absent: state.absent,
    absent_probes: state.absent_probes,
    reads: Cons { head: key, tail: state.reads },
    held: state.held
  }
}

fn acquisition_refused_lookup(state: AcquisitionState, lookup: ReferenceLookup) -> CandidateLookup {
  CandidateLookup { state: state, verdict: CandidateRefusedBy { lookup: lookup } }
}

// A decided probe: resolved becomes a fresh read; every other arm is a refusal carried whole.
fn acquisition_decided(state: AcquisitionState, directory: String, lookup: ReferenceLookup) -> CandidateLookup {
  match lookup {
    ReferenceResolved { module: module, member: _, file: file, content: content } =>
      CandidateLookup {
        state: acquisition_with_read(state: state, key: module_file_key(file: file)),
        verdict: CandidateFresh { module: module, file: file, host_directory: directory, content: content }
      }
    ReferenceUnknownModule { reference: _ } => acquisition_refused_lookup(state: state, lookup: lookup)
    ReferenceRefused { module: _, file: _, cause: _ } => acquisition_refused_lookup(state: state, lookup: lookup)
    ReferenceModuleLineMissing { module: _, file: _ } => acquisition_refused_lookup(state: state, lookup: lookup)
    ReferenceModuleLineDisagrees { module: _, declared: _, file: _ } => acquisition_refused_lookup(state: state, lookup: lookup)
    ReferenceModuleHeaderRefused { module: _, file: _, cause: _ } => acquisition_refused_lookup(state: state, lookup: lookup)
    ReferenceCrossTreeDenied { module: _, file: _, reason: _ } => acquisition_refused_lookup(state: state, lookup: lookup)
  }
}

// ONE REQUEST'S LOOKUP. The next candidate comes from the pure decision (the layout's file for it
// and the cross-tree direction, decided before any read). A candidate already acquired is decided
// without a read, and one already probed absent is skipped without one, so no file is read twice and
// no absence is probed twice. The cross-tree verdict precedes both shortcuts: a module acquired for a
// v2 caller is still denied to a dag caller.
fn acquisition_lookup(
  env: AcquisitionEnv,
  state: AcquisitionState,
  request: AcquisitionRequest,
  candidates: List<String>
) -> CandidateLookup {
  match reference_next_read(
    layout: env.layout,
    from_tree: request.from_tree,
    from_module: request.from_module,
    reference: request.spelling,
    candidates: candidates
  ) {
    ReferenceNextReadNone { reference: _ } => CandidateLookup { state: state, verdict: CandidateUnknown }
    ReferenceNextReadDenied { module: module, file: file, reason: reason } =>
      acquisition_refused_lookup(state: state, lookup: ReferenceCrossTreeDenied { module: module, file: file, reason: reason })
    ReferenceNextReadFile { module: module, file: file, rest: rest } =>
      if text_set_has(set: state.known, name: module) {
        CandidateLookup { state: state, verdict: CandidateKnown { module: module } }
      } else if text_set_has(set: state.absent, name: module_file_key(file: file)) {
        acquisition_lookup(env: env, state: state, request: request, candidates: rest)
      } else {
        match acquisition_candidate_probe(env: env, state: state, request: request, module: module, file: file, rest: rest) {
          CandidateProbeContinue { state: probed, rest: remaining } =>
            acquisition_lookup(env: env, state: probed, request: request, candidates: remaining)
          CandidateProbeSettled { lookup: settled } => settled
        }
      }
  }
}

// One candidate probe is either settled (a verdict) or an absence that sends the lookup on to the
// remaining candidates. The continuation is a VALUE the lookup acts on: this function does not call
// the lookup back, so the lookup is the only recursion and the emitter's tail-call lowering covers
// it (mutual recursion between effectful functions is not lowered).
type CandidateProbe
  = CandidateProbeContinue { state: AcquisitionState, rest: List<String> }
  | CandidateProbeSettled { lookup: CandidateLookup }

// A candidate the layout derives (a ledger's roster) is contributed by its derivation; every other
// candidate is one exact read of its file.
fn acquisition_candidate_probe(
  env: AcquisitionEnv,
  state: AcquisitionState,
  request: AcquisitionRequest,
  module: String,
  file: ModuleFileRef,
  rest: List<String>
) -> CandidateProbe {
  match acquisition_host_directory(bindings: env.bindings, tree: file.tree) {
    Absent => CandidateProbeSettled { lookup: CandidateLookup { state: state, verdict: CandidateTreeUnbound { tree: file.tree } } }
    Present { value: directory } =>
      match derived_roster_named(layout: env.layout, module: module) {
        Present { value: roster } =>
          acquisition_derive_candidate(state: state, roster: roster, module: module, file: file, directory: directory, rest: rest)
        Absent => acquisition_probe_candidate(env: env, state: state, request: request, module: module, file: file, directory: directory, rest: rest)
      }
  }
}

fn acquisition_probe_candidate(
  env: AcquisitionEnv,
  state: AcquisitionState,
  request: AcquisitionRequest,
  module: String,
  file: ModuleFileRef,
  directory: String,
  rest: List<String>
) -> CandidateProbe {
  match held_read_of(held: state.held, file: file) {
    HeldReadMisfiled { held: other } =>
      CandidateProbeSettled {
        lookup: acquisition_refused_lookup(state: state, lookup: ReferenceRefused { module: module, file: file, cause: held_misfiled_cause(file: file, held: other) })
      }
    HeldReadFor { read: held } =>
      match reference_after_probe(
        reader: acquisition_header_reader(env: env),
        reference: request.spelling,
        module: module,
        rest: rest,
        probe: ModuleFilePresent { file: held.file, content: held.content }
      ) {
        ReferenceContinue { rest: remaining } => CandidateProbeContinue { state: state, rest: remaining }
        ReferenceDecided { lookup: lookup } =>
          CandidateProbeSettled { lookup: acquisition_decided_held(state: state, directory: directory, lookup: lookup) }
      }
    HeldReadNone => {
      let read = acquisition_read_source(host_directory: directory, file: file)
      match reference_after_probe(
        reader: acquisition_header_reader(env: env),
        reference: request.spelling,
        module: module,
        rest: rest,
        probe: held_source_probe(read: read)
      ) {
        ReferenceContinue { rest: remaining } =>
          CandidateProbeContinue {
            state: acquisition_with_absent(state: state, key: module_file_key(file: file)),
            rest: remaining
          }
        ReferenceDecided { lookup: lookup } =>
          CandidateProbeSettled {
            lookup: acquisition_decided(state: acquisition_holding(state: state, layout: env.layout, module: module, read: read), directory: directory, lookup: lookup)
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

// A module whose file a derivation already read is decided from those bytes and records no second read:
// the read was recorded when the derivation made it.
fn acquisition_decided_held(state: AcquisitionState, directory: String, lookup: ReferenceLookup) -> CandidateLookup {
  match lookup {
    ReferenceResolved { module: module, member: _, file: file, content: content } =>
      CandidateLookup { state: state, verdict: CandidateFresh { module: module, file: file, host_directory: directory, content: content } }
    _ => acquisition_decided(state: state, directory: directory, lookup: lookup)
  }
}

// ---- contributing a derived roster ------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// THE ONE DIRECTORY A COMPILE LISTS, AND ONLY WHEN A REQUEST NAMES ITS ROSTER. A roster module has no
// file (v2.compiler.source_authority derived_roster_module), so its request is answered by listing the
// roster's own row directory, reading each sibling once, and rendering the roster from those bytes.
// Nothing else is walked: a compile that names no roster lists no directory (R9). The storage path
// is probed first and a file there REFUSES, located: it is never consumed (it may be stale against the
// rows) and never overridden (two sources for one module). A directory that does not exist is the
// roster being absent, and the lookup falls back to a shorter prefix exactly as for an absent file; any
// other failed listing or read refuses rather than shortening the roster.
//
// A sibling the acquisition already read is taken from its sealed read (AcquisitionState held) and not
// read again; any other sibling is read here, and its sealed read is held so a later request for it
// reads nothing.
fn acquisition_derive_refused(state: AcquisitionState, module: String, refusal: DerivedRosterRefusal) -> CandidateProbe {
  CandidateProbeSettled {
    lookup: CandidateLookup {
      state: state,
      verdict: CandidateDerivedRefused { refusal: AcquisitionDerivedRosterRefused { module: module, refusal: refusal } }
    }
  }
}

// The row directory of a roster, relative to its tree: the roster's storage path less its file name.
fn derived_roster_relative_directory(file: ModuleFileRef) -> String {
  substring(s: file.relative_path, start: 0, end: string_length(s: file.relative_path) - string_length(s: concat("/", derived_roster_stem(), ".dag")))
}

// THE ONE DERIVATION, shared by the demand route and the population route: the storage path probed and
// refused if a file occupies it, the row directory listed, every sibling's bytes taken from `held` or
// read, the members selected and the roster rendered (v2.compiler.source_authority derived_roster_select
// and derived_roster_text). The routes differ only in what they already hold and in what they do with
// the result: a compile holds the sealed reads of the siblings it already read (AcquisitionState
// held), a population job holds nothing; a compile records the reads this derivation made and admits
// the selected rows from these bytes, a population job carries the roster as one member.
// `held` is keyed by file key (v2.compiler.source_authority module_file_key), and every entry is a
// HeldSourceRead, so the bytes a derivation takes from it are a read of that file and nothing else.
type DerivedRosterDerivation
  = DerivedRosterDerived { content: String, inputs: List<String>, members: List<String>, siblings: List<DerivedSiblingRead> }
  | DerivedRosterAbsent
  | DerivedRosterDerivationRefused { refusal: DerivedRosterRefusal }

// One sibling as the derivation holds it; `fresh` when this derivation read it, rather than taking it from
// what the caller held.
type DerivedSiblingRead {
  module: String
  read: HeldSourceRead
  fresh: Bool
}

type DerivedSiblingsRead
  = DerivedSiblingsReadOk { siblings: List<DerivedSiblingRead> }
  | DerivedSiblingsReadRefused { refusal: DerivedRosterRefusal }

fn derived_sibling_file(roster_file: ModuleFileRef, stem: String) -> ModuleFileRef {
  ModuleFileRef { tree: roster_file.tree, relative_path: concat(derived_roster_relative_directory(file: roster_file), "/", stem, ".dag") }
}

fn derived_sibling_stem(roster: StorageDerivedRoster, sibling: DerivedSiblingRead) -> String {
  substring(s: sibling.module, start: string_length(s: roster.row_namespace) + 1, end: string_length(s: sibling.module))
}

// The reads are effects, so the walk over the stems is a self-recursion and not a fold: an effectful
// call inside a lambda is not lowered by the emitter.
fn derived_roster_read_siblings(
  roster: StorageDerivedRoster,
  roster_file: ModuleFileRef,
  host_directory: String,
  held: Map<String, HeldSourceRead>,
  pending: List<String>,
  read: List<DerivedSiblingRead>
) -> DerivedSiblingsRead {
  match list_head(xs: pending) {
    Absent => DerivedSiblingsReadOk { siblings: read }
    Present { value: stem } => {
      let file = derived_sibling_file(roster_file: roster_file, stem: stem)
      let module = derived_roster_row_module(roster: roster, stem: stem)
      match held_read_of(held: held, file: file) {
        HeldReadMisfiled { held: other } => DerivedSiblingsReadRefused { refusal: DerivedRosterSiblingMisfiled { file: file, held: other } }
        HeldReadFor { read: sibling } =>
          derived_roster_read_siblings(
            roster: roster, roster_file: roster_file, host_directory: host_directory, held: held,
            pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1),
            read: list_snoc_item(xs: read, item: DerivedSiblingRead { module: module, read: sibling, fresh: false })
          )
        HeldReadNone =>
          match acquisition_read_source(host_directory: host_directory, file: file) {
            HeldSourcePresent { read: sibling } =>
              derived_roster_read_siblings(
                roster: roster, roster_file: roster_file, host_directory: host_directory, held: held,
                pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1),
                read: list_snoc_item(xs: read, item: DerivedSiblingRead { module: module, read: sibling, fresh: true })
              )
            HeldSourceAbsent { file: _ } => DerivedSiblingsReadRefused { refusal: DerivedRosterSiblingUnread { file: file, cause: "the listing named the file and it is absent" } }
            HeldSourceRefused { file: _, cause: cause } => DerivedSiblingsReadRefused { refusal: DerivedRosterSiblingUnread { file: file, cause: cause } }
          }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn derived_roster_selected(roster: StorageDerivedRoster, siblings: List<DerivedSiblingRead>) -> DerivedRosterDerivation {
  match derived_roster_select(
    roster: roster,
    siblings: list_map(xs: siblings, f: fn(s) { DerivedRosterSibling { stem: derived_sibling_stem(roster: roster, sibling: s), content: s.read.content } })
  ) {
    DerivedRosterRowMisplaced { stem: stem, declared: declared } =>
      DerivedRosterDerivationRefused { refusal: DerivedRosterMisplacedRow { stem: stem, declared: declared } }
    DerivedRosterSelected { members: members } =>
      DerivedRosterDerived {
        content: derived_roster_text(roster: roster, members: members),
        inputs: list_map(xs: siblings, f: fn(s) { module_file_key(file: s.read.file) }),
        members: members,
        siblings: siblings
      }
  }
}

fn derived_roster_derive(roster: StorageDerivedRoster, file: ModuleFileRef, host_directory: String, held: Map<String, HeldSourceRead>) -> DerivedRosterDerivation {
  match acquisition_probe(host_directory: host_directory, file: file) {
    ModuleFilePresent { file: occupied, content: _ } => DerivedRosterDerivationRefused { refusal: DerivedRosterOccupied { file: occupied } }
    ModuleFileRefused { file: refused, cause: cause } => DerivedRosterDerivationRefused { refusal: DerivedRosterProbeRefused { file: refused, cause: cause } }
    ModuleFileAbsent { file: _ } =>
      match derived_roster_listing(directory: host_directory, relative: derived_roster_relative_directory(file: file)) {
        DerivedRosterListingAbsent => DerivedRosterAbsent {}
        DerivedRosterListingRefused { directory: unlisted, cause: cause } =>
          DerivedRosterDerivationRefused { refusal: DerivedRosterUnlisted { directory: unlisted, cause: cause } }
        DerivedRosterListed { stems: stems } =>
          match derived_roster_read_siblings(roster: roster, roster_file: file, host_directory: host_directory, held: held, pending: stems, read: []) {
            DerivedSiblingsReadRefused { refusal: refusal } => DerivedRosterDerivationRefused { refusal: refusal }
            DerivedSiblingsReadOk { siblings: siblings } => derived_roster_selected(roster: roster, siblings: siblings)
          }
      }
  }
}

// ---- the demand route's adapter ----

// Every read the derivation made is a read of the closure, recorded once, and its sealed read is held so
// a later request for that module reads nothing.
fn acquisition_with_derivation_reads(state: AcquisitionState, siblings: List<DerivedSiblingRead>) -> AcquisitionState {
  fold_list(xs: siblings, empty: state, cons: fn(acc, s) {
    if s.fresh { acquisition_with_held(state: acquisition_with_read(state: acc, key: module_file_key(file: s.read.file)), read: s.read) } else { acc }
  })
}

// The selected rows no request has admitted yet: they are admitted from the derivation's bytes, before
// the roster, so the roster's own imports of them read nothing.
fn derived_rows_to_admit(state: AcquisitionState, roster: StorageDerivedRoster, siblings: List<DerivedSiblingRead>, members: List<String>) -> List<DerivedRowRead> {
  let selected = text_set_of(names: members)
  fold_list(xs: siblings, empty: [], cons: fn(acc, sibling) {
    if !text_set_has(set: state.known, name: sibling.module) && text_set_has(set: selected, name: derived_sibling_stem(roster: roster, sibling: sibling)) {
      list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: DerivedRowRead { module: sibling.module, file: sibling.read.file, content: sibling.read.content })
    } else {
      acc
    }
  })
}

fn acquisition_derive_candidate(
  state: AcquisitionState,
  roster: StorageDerivedRoster,
  module: String,
  file: ModuleFileRef,
  directory: String,
  rest: List<String>
) -> CandidateProbe {
  match derived_roster_derive(roster: roster, file: file, host_directory: directory, held: state.held) {
    DerivedRosterAbsent => CandidateProbeContinue { state: acquisition_with_absent(state: state, key: module_file_key(file: file)), rest: rest }
    DerivedRosterDerivationRefused { refusal: refusal } => acquisition_derive_refused(state: state, module: module, refusal: refusal)
    DerivedRosterDerived { content: content, inputs: inputs, members: members, siblings: siblings } => {
      let read = acquisition_with_derivation_reads(state: state, siblings: siblings)
      CandidateProbeSettled {
        lookup: CandidateLookup {
          state: read,
          verdict: CandidateDerived {
            module: module,
            file: file,
            host_directory: directory,
            content: content,
            rows: derived_rows_to_admit(state: read, roster: roster, siblings: siblings, members: members),
            inputs: inputs
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

// THE ROW DIRECTORY'S DIRECT `.dag` CHILDREN, by stem, sorted: the siblings of a roster. A subdirectory's
// modules are not siblings (they are rows of a deeper namespace), and the roster's own stem is not one.
type DerivedRosterListing
  = DerivedRosterListed { stems: List<String> }
  | DerivedRosterListingAbsent
  | DerivedRosterListingRefused { directory: String, cause: String }

fn derived_roster_listing(directory: String, relative: String) -> DerivedRosterListing {
  let row_directory = source_root_child_path(directory: directory, name: relative)
  match source_root_walk(pending: [SourceRootWalkTask { path: row_directory, root: true, origin: row_directory }], paths: [], suffix: ".dag") {
    SourceRootPathsRefused { path: path, kind: kind, cause: cause } =>
      if namespace_directory_is_the_absent_path(path: path, directory: row_directory, kind: kind) {
        DerivedRosterListingAbsent {}
      } else {
        DerivedRosterListingRefused { directory: row_directory, cause: concat(path, ": ", kind, ": ", cause) }
      }
    SourceRootPathsRead { paths: paths } =>
      DerivedRosterListed { stems: derived_roster_direct_stems(row_directory: row_directory, paths: list_map(xs: paths, f: fn(walked) { walked.path })) }
  }
}

fn derived_roster_direct_stems(row_directory: String, paths: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  sort_by(derived_roster_listed_stems(row_directory: row_directory, paths: paths), stem => stem)
}

fn derived_roster_listed_stems(row_directory: String, paths: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  let prefix_length = string_length(s: source_root_child_path(directory: row_directory, name: ""))
  fold_list(xs: paths, empty: [], cons: fn(acc, path) {
    let rest = substring(s: path, start: prefix_length, end: string_length(s: path))
    let stem = substring(s: rest, start: 0, end: string_length(s: rest) - 4)
    if string_contains(s: rest, pattern: "/") || stem == derived_roster_stem() {
      acc
    } else {
      list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: stem)
    }
  })
}

// ---- reading what a member asks for -------------------------------------------------------------

// A CODE-POSITION QUALIFIED NAME IS A SPINE OF TWO OR MORE SEGMENTS, not a string that happens to
// contain dots. Flattening the spine and splitting the lexeme again treats a single interned
// string-literal payload (`"test.claim.spark.foo"` as one atom) as a module reference, which is
// how identity strings drove acquisition. A bare name (one segment) is not requestable (R1), even
// when that segment's lexeme contains `.`.
fn acquisition_qn_names_a_module(qn: QualifiedName) -> Bool {
  match qn {
    Empty => false
    Cons { head: _, tail: rest } =>
      match rest {
        Empty => false
        Cons { head: _, tail: _ } => true
      }
  }
}

fn acquisition_import_texts(rows: List<Import>) -> List<String> {
  fold_list(xs: rows, empty: [], cons: fn(acc, row) { list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: qualified_name_to_dotted_string(qn: row.target)) })
}

// A binding row whose module an import already names is covered by that import; the others (an alias
// to a member of a module no import names) are requests of their own, split by prefix.
fn acquisition_uncovered_binding_texts(tree: NormalizedTree, imported: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  let covered = text_set_of(names: imported)
  fold_list(xs: tree.import_bindings, empty: [], cons: fn(acc, row) {
    if text_set_has(set: covered, name: qualified_name_to_dotted_string(qn: qualified_name_init(qn: row.target))) {
      acc
    } else {
      list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: qualified_name_to_dotted_string(qn: row.target))
    }
  })
}

// A reference site's spelling, kept once per module, when the site can name a module at all. A one-segment
// spelling is a bare name and is not requestable (R1). A spelling whose head an enclosing binder or a
// module-level value declaration binds is a projection off that binder and names no module, so it requests
// nothing: whether a spelling is a module reference is decided by the lexical frames at its site
// (v2.compiler.lexical_scope, through v2.compiler.reference_site_collector), the same frames resolution reads,
// and never by the spelling.
fn acquisition_reference_texts(collection: ScopedReferenceSiteCollection) -> List<String> {
  fold_list(
    xs: collection.sites,
    empty: AcquisitionSeen { order: [], seen: empty_map() },
    cons: fn(acc, scoped) { acquisition_seen_site(acc: acc, scoped: scoped) }
  ).order
}

// Only a head nothing binds, or a head no VALUE binder could bind, is a candidate module reference. A head a
// value binder binds is a projection; a head the walk could not classify (HeadBindingUndecided) might be one,
// and probing storage for it could deny a valid program or read a file the program does not mention, so
// neither of those requests anything or reads anything.
// THE BINDING FACT, ALONE -- the position is deliberately NOT consulted here (tidy-ant-251's D3
// ruling). Acquisition asks "could this head be a module's member?", which a value frame answers: an
// unbound head is the only reading with no local explanation, so it is the only candidate. Reading the
// POSITION here is what would widen acquisition: an imported qualified tag (`Choice.Yes` with Choice
// imported) sits at a label position, and "non-value position => candidate" would make a head the tree
// already says is undecided into a module request. The roster reads BOTH fields instead, because it
// asks the different question "would importing this name be wrong?".
//
// The two consumers therefore never re-decide anything: each reads one fact that ONE authority issued,
// and the claim that a non-value head still requests its module is carried by the head reading (a type
// mention in a signature is not a value binder's name, so it reads unbound), not by a position arm --
// which is why v2.test.claim.module_acquisition's binder-type and annotation positives stay green here.
fn acquisition_head_is_candidate_module(head: SiteHeadBinding) -> Bool {
  match head {
    HeadLexicallyUnbound => true
    HeadBoundInFrame => false
    HeadBoundAtRootValue => false
    HeadBindingUndecided => false
  }
}

fn acquisition_seen_site(acc: AcquisitionSeen, scoped: ScopedReferenceSite) -> AcquisitionSeen {
  let text = qualified_name_to_dotted_string(qn: scoped.site.spelling)
  if acquisition_head_is_candidate_module(head: scoped.head) && acquisition_qn_names_a_module(qn: scoped.site.spelling) && !text_set_has(set: acc.seen, name: text) {
    AcquisitionSeen {
      order: list_snoc_item(xs: acc.order, item: text),
      seen: map_insert(m: acc.seen, key: text, value: true)
    }
  } else {
    acc
  }
}

fn acquisition_read_requests(
  front: AcquisitionFrontEnd,
  sites: ScopedReferenceSiteCollection,
  module: String,
  file: ModuleFileRef
) -> AcquisitionMemberRead {
  match admission_from_module_root(root: front.parse_root) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } =>
      AcquisitionMemberReadRefused { refusal: AcquisitionMemberRefused { module: module, file: file, diagnostics: d } }
    Accepted { value: admission, diagnostics: _ } =>
      acquisition_requests_ok(front: front, sites: sites, imported: acquisition_import_texts(rows: admission.imports))
  }
}

// `T?` is sugar for the declaration std.optional.Optional, so a member that writes it OWES that declaration: the
// site is promoted from a qualified reference (whose unknown module requests nothing) to an imported member (whose
// unknown module refuses). An authored reference spelled the same is owed the same declaration.
fn acquisition_sugar_member_texts(references: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  let sugar = qualified_name_to_dotted_string(qn: optional_declaration_identity())
  match list_find_first(xs: references, predicate: fn(t) { t == sugar }) {
    Present { value: _ } => [sugar]
    Absent => []
  }
}

// A list literal, a string-keyed map literal and a string template each lower to a reference to a declaration
// the source never spells, so they OWE it the way `T?` does: every path the tree's generated heads name is an
// imported member, whose unknown module refuses, and not a reference site, which requests nothing
// (v2.compiler.generated_head_demand). Never an authored import and never an ambient built-in.
fn acquisition_generated_head_member_texts(root: Node) -> List<String> {
  list_map(xs: generated_head_demand(root: root), f: fn(path) { qualified_name_to_dotted_string(qn: path) })
}

fn acquisition_requests_ok(front: AcquisitionFrontEnd, sites: ScopedReferenceSiteCollection, imported: List<String>) -> AcquisitionMemberRead {
  let references = acquisition_reference_texts(collection: sites)
  AcquisitionMemberReadOk {
    requests: MemberRequests {
      imports: imported,
      members: concat(acquisition_uncovered_binding_texts(tree: front.tree, imported: imported), acquisition_sugar_member_texts(references: references), acquisition_generated_head_member_texts(root: front.parse_root)),
      references: references
    },
    test_markers: list_map(xs: front.tree.test_markers.marked, f: fn(marked) { symbol_lexeme(sym: marked.name) })
  }
}

// Tokenize and parse, which is all the population parse's subject needs and the first two thirds of the
// front end a closure member needs.
fn acquisition_parsed(env: AcquisitionEnv, file: ModuleFileRef, content: String) -> Outcome<ParseArtifact> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: tokenize_prepared(text: content, file: symbol_intern_lexeme(lexeme: module_file_key(file: file)), prepared: env.lex),
    f: fn(tokens) { parse_module_prepared(tokens: tokens, prepared: env.grammar) }
  )
}

fn acquisition_front_end(env: AcquisitionEnv, file: ModuleFileRef, content: String) -> Outcome<AcquisitionFrontEnd> {
  bind_outcome(
    o: acquisition_parsed(env: env, file: file, content: content),
    f: fn(artifact) {
      bind_outcome(
        o: normalize(parse_tree: artifact.tree),
        f: fn(tree) { outcome_accepted(value: AcquisitionFrontEnd { parse_root: artifact.tree, tree: tree }) }
      )
    }
  )
}

// WHETHER ONE MEMBER PARSES, and nothing retained of it: the verdict a streaming population parse
// folds, carrying the cause and never the tree.
type MemberParse
  = MemberParses
  | MemberParseRefused { cause: String, at: Optional<String> }

// WHERE THE FATAL DIAGNOSTIC POINTS, as the census's `<line>:<byte column>` (v2.workflow.source_locus, the single
// rendering authority). The parse stage already locates its refusal (a byte range in the interned file key);
// the verdict used to keep only the reason, so a refused member was found by bisecting the population.
// A diagnostic with no byte range is Absent (std.optional, the one absence authority), never a sentinel string.
fn acquisition_refusal_at(d: NonEmptyDiagnostics, content: String) -> Optional<String> {
  match diagnostics_fatal(d: d).at {
    SourcePortLocus { anchor: _, file: _, extent: ByteRange { start: s, end: _ } } => optional_present(value: locus_at(starts: line_starts(text: content), offset: s))
    Textual { file: _, extent: ByteRange { start: s, end: _ } } => optional_present(value: locus_at(starts: line_starts(text: content), offset: s))
    _ => optional_absent()
  }
}

fn acquisition_parse(env: AcquisitionEnv, file: ModuleFileRef, content: String) -> MemberParse {
  match acquisition_parsed(env: env, file: file, content: content) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } => MemberParseRefused { cause: symbol_lexeme(sym: diagnostics_fatal_reason(d: d)), at: acquisition_refusal_at(d: d, content: content) }
    Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => MemberParses {}
  }
}

// THE MARKED DECLARATIONS OF ONE MEMBER, read from its parse tree alone. A test is a declaration carrying the
// authored `test` marker, and the marker channel (v2.compiler.test_marker_capture) is a pure reader over
// the parse tree -- the same capture normalize runs beside lowering -- so the declarations a file marks do not
// wait on lowering. That is what lets a job whose subject is every governed file ask this of a module
// that cannot be lowered in isolation (a type declaring one variant twice
// refuses ordinary normalization, v2.compiler.normalize), without a second reading of the marker and
// without reading an unlowerable file as an unmarked one: a file that does not parse, or whose shell the
// capture cannot read, is a refusal and never an empty list.
//
// Each declaration carries its authored content beside its name (test_marker_capture TestMarkedContent), so a
// job that compares two readings of a file pairs what a claim says and not only what it is called.
type AcquisitionMarkedDeclaration {
  name: String
  content: String
}

type AcquisitionMarkerRead
  = AcquisitionMarkersRead { declarations: List<AcquisitionMarkedDeclaration> }
  | AcquisitionMarkersRefused { cause: String }

fn acquisition_test_markers(env: AcquisitionEnv, file: ModuleFileRef, content: String) -> AcquisitionMarkerRead {
  match acquisition_parsed(env: env, file: file, content: content) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } => AcquisitionMarkersRefused { cause: symbol_lexeme(sym: diagnostics_fatal_reason(d: d)) }
    Accepted { value: artifact, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match test_marker_content(tree: artifact.tree) {
        Rejected { diagnostics: d } => AcquisitionMarkersRefused { cause: symbol_lexeme(sym: diagnostics_fatal_reason(d: d)) }
        Accepted { value: marked, diagnostics: _ } =>
          AcquisitionMarkersRead {
            declarations: list_map(xs: marked, f: fn(m) { AcquisitionMarkedDeclaration { name: symbol_lexeme(sym: m.name), content: m.content } })
          }
      }
  }
}

// The member's sites are collected ONCE: the totality check and the requests read the same collection.
fn acquisition_sites_requests(
  front: AcquisitionFrontEnd,
  sites: ScopedReferenceSiteCollection,
  module: String,
  file: ModuleFileRef
) -> AcquisitionMemberRead {
  if scoped_reference_site_collection_is_total(collection: sites) {
    acquisition_read_requests(front: front, sites: sites, module: module, file: file)
  } else {
    AcquisitionMemberReadRefused { refusal: AcquisitionMemberSitesUnreadable { module: module, file: file } }
  }
}

// The lookup admitted this file because its header read as the requested module; the admit asserts
// the same thing of the FULL parse, so a file whose whole parse names another module is refused rather
// than carried under the requested name.
fn acquisition_identity_checked(front: AcquisitionFrontEnd, module: String, file: ModuleFileRef) -> AcquisitionMemberRead {
  match qualified_name_from_module_node(root: front.parse_root) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } =>
      AcquisitionMemberReadRefused { refusal: AcquisitionMemberRefused { module: module, file: file, diagnostics: d } }
    Accepted { value: parsed, diagnostics: _ } =>
      if qualified_name_to_dotted_string(qn: parsed) == module {
        acquisition_sites_requests(
          front: front,
          sites: collect_scoped_module_reference_sites(
            root: front.tree.root,
            imported: list_map(xs: front.tree.import_bindings, f: fn(row) { row.binding })
          ),
          module: module,
          file: file
        )
      } else {
        AcquisitionMemberReadRefused {
          refusal: AcquisitionMemberIdentityDisagrees { module: module, file: file, declared: qualified_name_to_dotted_string(qn: parsed) }
        }
      }
  }
}

// THE IMPORT-DEPTH MEMBER READ: parse only; the module line is checked exactly as the full read checks it, and the
// requests are the import rows the full read already extracts (admission_from_module_root), never a second reader.
fn acquisition_import_requests(env: AcquisitionEnv, module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, content: String) -> AcquisitionMemberRead {
  match acquisition_parsed(env: env, file: file, content: content) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } =>
      AcquisitionMemberReadRefused { refusal: AcquisitionMemberRefused { module: module, file: file, diagnostics: d } }
    Accepted { value: artifact, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match qualified_name_from_module_node(root: artifact.tree) {
        Rejected { diagnostics: d } =>
          AcquisitionMemberReadRefused { refusal: AcquisitionMemberRefused { module: module, file: file, diagnostics: d } }
        Accepted { value: parsed, diagnostics: _ } =>
          if qualified_name_to_dotted_string(qn: parsed) == module {
            match admission_from_module_root(root: artifact.tree) {
              Rejected { diagnostics: d } =>
                AcquisitionMemberReadRefused { refusal: AcquisitionMemberRefused { module: module, file: file, diagnostics: d } }
              Accepted { value: admission, diagnostics: _ } =>
                AcquisitionMemberReadOk {
                  requests: MemberRequests { imports: acquisition_import_texts(rows: admission.imports), members: [], references: [] },
                  test_markers: []
                }
            }
          } else {
            AcquisitionMemberReadRefused {
              refusal: AcquisitionMemberIdentityDisagrees { module: module, file: file, declared: qualified_name_to_dotted_string(qn: parsed) }
            }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn acquisition_member_requests(env: AcquisitionEnv, module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, content: String) -> AcquisitionMemberRead {
  match env.depth {
    AcquireImportClosure => acquisition_import_requests(env: env, module: module, file: file, content: content)
    AcquireFullClosure => acquisition_full_requests(env: env, module: module, file: file, content: content)
  }
}

fn acquisition_full_requests(env: AcquisitionEnv, module: String, file: ModuleFileRef, content: String) -> AcquisitionMemberRead {
  match acquisition_front_end(env: env, file: file, content: content) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } =>
      AcquisitionMemberReadRefused { refusal: AcquisitionMemberRefused { module: module, file: file, diagnostics: d } }
    Accepted { value: front, diagnostics: _ } => acquisition_identity_checked(front: front, module: module, file: file)
  }
}

fn acquisition_requests_of(module: String, tree: SourceRootRef, texts: List<String>, kind: AcquisitionKind) -> List<AcquisitionRequest> {
  list_map(xs: texts, f: fn(text) {
    AcquisitionRequest { from_module: module, from_tree: tree, spelling: text, kind: kind }
  })
}

fn acquisition_member_request_list(module: String, tree: SourceRootRef, requests: MemberRequests) -> List<AcquisitionRequest> {
  concat(
    acquisition_requests_of(module: module, tree: tree, texts: requests.imports, kind: AcquireImportedModule),
    acquisition_requests_of(module: module, tree: tree, texts: requests.members, kind: AcquireImportedMember),
    acquisition_requests_of(module: module, tree: tree, texts: requests.references, kind: AcquireQualifiedReference)
  )
}

// ---- one step -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn acquisition_admitted(
  state: AcquisitionState,
  module: String,
  file: ModuleFileRef,
  host_directory: String,
  content: String,
  own: List<AcquisitionRequest>,
  test_markers: List<String>,
  origin: AcquiredOrigin
) -> AcquisitionStep {
  AcquisitionAdvanced {
    state: AcquisitionState {
      pending: concat(own, state.pending),
      modules: Cons {
        head: AcquiredModule { name: module, file: file, host_directory: host_directory, content: content, requests: own, test_markers: test_markers, origin: origin },
        tail: state.modules
      },
      known: map_insert(m: state.known, key: module, value: true),
      absent: state.absent,
      absent_probes: state.absent_probes,
      reads: state.reads,
      held: state.held
    }
  }
}

fn acquisition_admit(
  env: AcquisitionEnv,
  state: AcquisitionState,
  module: String,
  file: ModuleFileRef,
  host_directory: String,
  content: String,
  origin: AcquiredOrigin
) -> AcquisitionStep {
  match acquisition_member_requests(env: env, module: module, file: file, content: content) {
    AcquisitionMemberReadRefused { refusal: refusal } => AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: refusal, state: state }
    AcquisitionMemberReadOk { requests: requests, test_markers: markers } =>
      acquisition_admitted(
        state: state,
        module: module,
        file: file,
        host_directory: host_directory,
        content: content,
        own: acquisition_member_request_list(module: module, tree: file.tree, requests: requests),
        test_markers: markers,
        origin: origin
      )
  }
}

// An import, member or entry that names no module refuses; a qualified reference that names none
// requests nothing, because whether it is a module reference at all is the resolver's decision.
fn acquisition_unknown_step(state: AcquisitionState, request: AcquisitionRequest) -> AcquisitionStep {
  match request.kind {
    AcquireQualifiedReference => AcquisitionAdvanced { state: state }
    AcquireEntry => AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: AcquisitionUnknownModule { request: request }, state: state }
    AcquireImportedModule => AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: AcquisitionUnknownModule { request: request }, state: state }
    AcquireImportedMember => AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: AcquisitionUnknownModule { request: request }, state: state }
  }
}

fn acquisition_verdict_step(env: AcquisitionEnv, request: AcquisitionRequest, looked: CandidateLookup) -> AcquisitionStep {
  match looked.verdict {
    CandidateKnown { module: _ } => AcquisitionAdvanced { state: looked.state }
    CandidateUnknown => acquisition_unknown_step(state: looked.state, request: request)
    CandidateRefusedBy { lookup: lookup } =>
      AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: AcquisitionLookupRefused { request: request, lookup: lookup }, state: looked.state }
    CandidateTreeUnbound { tree: tree } =>
      AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: AcquisitionTreeUnbound { tree: source_root_ref_label(tree: tree), request: request }, state: looked.state }
    CandidateFresh { module: module, file: file, host_directory: directory, content: content } =>
      acquisition_admit(env: env, state: looked.state, module: module, file: file, host_directory: directory, content: content, origin: AcquiredFromFile)
    CandidateDerivedRefused { refusal: refusal } => AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: refusal, state: looked.state }
    CandidateDerived { module: module, file: file, host_directory: directory, content: content, rows: rows, inputs: inputs } =>
      acquisition_admit_derived(env: env, state: looked.state, module: module, file: file, host_directory: directory, content: content, rows: rows, inputs: inputs)
  }
}

// THE ROWS FIRST, THEN THE ROSTER. Each selected row the derivation read and no request has yet admitted
// is admitted from the bytes the derivation read, so the roster's own imports of its rows find them
// known and read nothing. A row that does not front-end refuses at the row, located, before the roster
// is admitted.
fn acquisition_admit_derived(
  env: AcquisitionEnv,
  state: AcquisitionState,
  module: String,
  file: ModuleFileRef,
  host_directory: String,
  content: String,
  rows: List<DerivedRowRead>,
  inputs: List<String>
) -> AcquisitionStep {
  match acquisition_admit_rows(env: env, step: AcquisitionAdvanced { state: state }, rows: rows, host_directory: host_directory) {
    AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: refusal, state: stopped } => AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: refusal, state: stopped }
    AcquisitionAdvanced { state: admitted } =>
      acquisition_admit(env: env, state: admitted, module: module, file: file, host_directory: host_directory, content: content, origin: AcquiredDerivedRoster { inputs: inputs })
  }
}

fn acquisition_admit_rows(env: AcquisitionEnv, step: AcquisitionStep, rows: List<DerivedRowRead>, host_directory: String) -> AcquisitionStep {
  match step {
    AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: _, state: _ } => step
    AcquisitionAdvanced { state: state } =>
      match list_head(xs: rows) {
        Absent => step
        Present { value: row } =>
          acquisition_admit_rows(
            env: env,
            step: acquisition_admit(env: env, state: state, module: row.module, file: row.file, host_directory: host_directory, content: row.content, origin: AcquiredFromFile),
            rows: skip(xs: rows, n: 1),
            host_directory: host_directory
          )
      }
  }
}

fn acquisition_step(env: AcquisitionEnv, state: AcquisitionState, request: AcquisitionRequest) -> AcquisitionStep {
  acquisition_verdict_step(
    env: env,
    request: request,
    looked: acquisition_lookup(env: env, state: state, request: request, candidates: acquisition_candidates(request: request))
  )
}

fn acquisition_popped(state: AcquisitionState) -> AcquisitionState {
  AcquisitionState {
    pending: skip(xs: state.pending, n: 1),
    modules: state.modules,
    known: state.known,
    absent: state.absent,
    absent_probes: state.absent_probes,
    reads: state.reads,
    held: state.held
  }
}

fn acquisition_completed(state: AcquisitionState, entries: List<String>) -> Acquisition {
  AcquisitionComplete {
    entries: entries,
    modules: list_reverse(xs: state.modules),
    reads: list_reverse(xs: state.reads),
    absent_probes: state.absent_probes
  }
}

fn acquisition_loop(env: AcquisitionEnv, state: AcquisitionState, entries: List<String>) -> Acquisition {
  match list_head(xs: state.pending) {
    Absent => acquisition_completed(state: state, entries: entries)
    Present { value: request } =>
      match acquisition_step(env: env, state: acquisition_popped(state: state), request: request) {
        AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: refusal, state: stopped } =>
          AcquisitionRefused { refusal: refusal, reads: list_reverse(xs: stopped.reads) }
        AcquisitionAdvanced { state: next } => acquisition_loop(env: env, state: next, entries: entries)
      }
  }
}

// The initial requests, one entry request per entry in the order given. An entry the layout cannot
// derive a file for refuses the whole acquisition before any read.
type AcquisitionEntries
  = AcquisitionEntriesDerived { requests: List<AcquisitionRequest> }
  | AcquisitionEntriesRefused { refusal: AcquisitionRefusal }

fn acquisition_entry_requests(
  layout: SourceStorageLayout,
  pending: List<String>,
  acc: List<AcquisitionRequest>
) -> AcquisitionEntries {
  match list_head(xs: pending) {
    Absent => AcquisitionEntriesDerived { requests: acc }
    Present { value: entry } =>
      match module_file(layout: layout, name: entry) {
        ModuleNameUnderivable { name: name, cause: cause } =>
          AcquisitionEntriesRefused { refusal: AcquisitionEntryUnderivable { entry: name, cause: cause } }
        ModuleFileDerived { file: file } =>
          acquisition_entry_requests(
            layout: layout,
            pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1),
            acc: list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: AcquisitionRequest { from_module: "", from_tree: file.tree, spelling: entry, kind: AcquireEntry })
          )
      }
  }
}

fn acquisition_start(env: AcquisitionEnv, entries: List<String>) -> Acquisition {
  match acquisition_entry_requests(layout: env.layout, pending: entries, acc: []) {
    AcquisitionEntriesRefused { refusal: refusal } => AcquisitionRefused { refusal: refusal, reads: [] }
    AcquisitionEntriesDerived { requests: requests } =>
      acquisition_loop(
        env: env,
        state: AcquisitionState { pending: requests, modules: [], known: empty_map(), absent: empty_map(), absent_probes: 0, reads: [], held: empty_map() },
        entries: entries
      )
  }
}

// THE FRONT END, PREPARED ONCE: the dag grammar and lexical rules every acquisition and every
// population job parses with. A grammar that does not prepare is a refusal carried whole, never a
// front end that parses nothing.
type AcquisitionEnvPrepared
  = AcquisitionEnvReady { env: AcquisitionEnv }
  | AcquisitionEnvUnprepared { diagnostics: NonEmptyDiagnostics }

fn acquisition_env(layout: SourceStorageLayout, bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>) -> AcquisitionEnvPrepared {
  match prepare_grammar(grammar: dag_language_model().grammar) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } => AcquisitionEnvUnprepared { diagnostics: d }
    Accepted { value: prepared, diagnostics: _ } =>
      AcquisitionEnvReady {
        env: acquisition_env_over(layout: layout, bindings: bindings, lex: prepare_lex_rules(rules: dag_language_model().lex), grammar: prepared)
      }
  }
}

// THE ENV OVER A PREPARED LEXER AND GRAMMAR: the one place the header production's tail is derived
// from the grammar (v2.compiler.module_header module_header_tail), so it is carried once per run and
// every env built through it holds the tail its own grammar gives.
fn acquisition_env_over(
  layout: SourceStorageLayout,
  bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>,
  lex: PreparedLexRules,
  grammar: PreparedGrammar
) -> AcquisitionEnv {
  AcquisitionEnv { layout: layout, bindings: bindings, lex: lex, grammar: grammar, header_tail: module_header_tail(grammar: grammar), depth: AcquireFullClosure }
}

// The header reader over the env's prepared lexer and grammar: the one reading of a file's declared
// module (v2.compiler.module_header), shared by the lookup's agreement check and the layout verb.
fn acquisition_header_reader(env: AcquisitionEnv) -> ModuleHeaderReader {
  ModuleHeaderReader { lex: env.lex, grammar: env.grammar, tail: env.header_tail }
}

// THE DOOR: the closure of the entry modules under the layout, read through the bindings. One
// acquisition for several entries shares its known and absent memos, so a module two entries both
// reach is read once.
fn acquire_closure_of(layout: SourceStorageLayout, bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>, entries: List<String>) -> Acquisition {
  match acquisition_env(layout: layout, bindings: bindings) {
    AcquisitionEnvUnprepared { diagnostics: d } => AcquisitionRefused { refusal: AcquisitionGrammarUnprepared { diagnostics: d }, reads: [] }
    AcquisitionEnvReady { env: env } => acquisition_start(env: env, entries: entries)
  }
}

// THE IMPORT-GRAPH DOOR: the closure of `entries` over IMPORT edges only, each member parsed and nothing more.
fn acquire_import_closure_of(layout: SourceStorageLayout, bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>, entries: List<String>) -> Acquisition {
  match acquisition_env(layout: layout, bindings: bindings) {
    AcquisitionEnvUnprepared { diagnostics: d } => AcquisitionRefused { refusal: AcquisitionGrammarUnprepared { diagnostics: d }, reads: [] }
    AcquisitionEnvReady { env: env } =>
      acquisition_start(env: AcquisitionEnv { layout: env.layout, bindings: env.bindings, lex: env.lex, grammar: env.grammar, header_tail: env.header_tail, depth: AcquireImportClosure }, entries: entries)
  }
}

fn acquire_import_verified(layout: SourceStorageLayout, bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>, entries: List<String>) -> VerifiedAcquisition {
  acquisition_verified(acquisition: acquire_import_closure_of(layout: layout, bindings: bindings, entries: entries))
}

fn acquire_closure(layout: SourceStorageLayout, bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>, entry: String) -> Acquisition {
  acquire_closure_of(layout: layout, bindings: bindings, entries: [entry])
}

// ---- the census door: a closure that records where it is blocked ------------------------------

// THE CENSUS ACQUIRES THE SAME CLOSURE THE COMPILER DOES, BUT DOES NOT STOP AT A BLOCK. The compiler's
// door (acquisition_loop) refuses at the first refusal, because a compiler cannot build past an
// unreadable member. A census exists to report every such member, so this loop runs the SAME lookup
// and the SAME verdict step and, where the step refuses at one member or one edge, records the
// refusal and continues with the rest of the frontier. What a blocked member would have requested is
// unobserved, and the result says so: CensusAcquisitionBlocked is "acquired this far; behind these,
// unobserved", never a smaller population presented as closed.
// CONSUMERS: v2.test.claim.census_scoped_acquisition over planted closures, and the emitted driver's
// census-scoped verb (v2.compiler.source_root_eval), run by //gunbc/instruments:v2-native-census-scoped.
// The refusal is carried WHOLE -- its arm is its meaning (R3); this door adds no classification of
// its own beyond whether a refusal is local to one member, local to one edge, or ends the run.
// The refusals local to one member, and only those: a member block cannot hold an edge's or a
// run's refusal (DESIGN 5, unwritable). Each arm is the AcquisitionRefusal arm of the same name less
// the module, which the block carries once.
type CensusMemberRefusal
  = CensusMemberFrontEndRefused { file: ModuleFileRef, diagnostics: NonEmptyDiagnostics }
  | CensusMemberSitesUnreadable { file: ModuleFileRef }
  | CensusMemberIdentityDisagrees { file: ModuleFileRef, declared: String }

// The three refusals local to one edge, and only those: an edge block cannot hold a member's or a
// run's refusal (DESIGN 5). Each arm is the AcquisitionRefusal arm of the same name and fields.
type CensusEdgeRefusal
  = CensusEdgeUnknownModule { request: AcquisitionRequest }
  | CensusEdgeLookupRefused { request: AcquisitionRequest, lookup: ReferenceLookup }
  | CensusEdgeTreeUnbound { tree: String, request: AcquisitionRequest }

type CensusBlock
  = CensusMemberBlock {
      module: String
      refusal: CensusMemberRefusal
      // The bytes the attempted read returned, so the census reports the member from the read
      // acquisition made and never from a second one (R1).
      content: String
      host_directory: String
      // Every request that reached this member, in arrival order. A diamond onto one malformed
      // member is one block with two requesters, not two reads (R2).
      requesters: List<AcquisitionRequest>
    }
  | CensusEdgeBlock { refusal: CensusEdgeRefusal }

type CensusAcquisition
  = CensusAcquisitionClosed { entries: List<String>, modules: List<AcquiredModule>, reads: List<String> }
  | CensusAcquisitionBlocked {
      entries: List<String>
      modules: List<AcquiredModule>
      blocked_first: CensusBlock
      blocked_rest: List<CensusBlock>
      reads: List<String>
    }
  | CensusAcquisitionRefused { refusal: AcquisitionRefusal }

type CensusAcquisitionState {
  state: AcquisitionState
  // Blocked members by name, and every block, newest first (each block's requesters likewise newest
  // first); census_acquisition_finished reverses both once. The map holds the block's ordinal in
  // recording order, and block_count the number recorded, so a later requester reaches the one block
  // it names at position block_count - 1 - ordinal from the head.
  blocked_members: Map<String, Int>
  blocks: List<CensusBlock>
  block_count: Int
}

// Which refusals are local. A refusal that is local to neither (grammar unprepared, entry
// underivable) ends the census acquisition exactly as it ends the compiler's.
type CensusBlockLocality
  = CensusBlockedMember { module: String, refusal: CensusMemberRefusal }
  | CensusBlockedEdge { refusal: CensusEdgeRefusal }
  | CensusBlockNotLocal

fn census_block_locality(refusal: AcquisitionRefusal) -> CensusBlockLocality {
  match refusal {
    AcquisitionMemberRefused { module: m, file: f, diagnostics: d } =>
      CensusBlockedMember { module: m, refusal: CensusMemberFrontEndRefused { file: f, diagnostics: d } }
    AcquisitionMemberSitesUnreadable { module: m, file: f } =>
      CensusBlockedMember { module: m, refusal: CensusMemberSitesUnreadable { file: f } }
    AcquisitionMemberIdentityDisagrees { module: m, file: f, declared: d } =>
      CensusBlockedMember { module: m, refusal: CensusMemberIdentityDisagrees { file: f, declared: d } }
    AcquisitionUnknownModule { request: q } => CensusBlockedEdge { refusal: CensusEdgeUnknownModule { request: q } }
    AcquisitionLookupRefused { request: q, lookup: l } => CensusBlockedEdge { refusal: CensusEdgeLookupRefused { request: q, lookup: l } }
    AcquisitionTreeUnbound { tree: t, request: q } => CensusBlockedEdge { refusal: CensusEdgeTreeUnbound { tree: t, request: q } }
    AcquisitionGrammarUnprepared { diagnostics: _ } => CensusBlockNotLocal
    AcquisitionEntryUnderivable { entry: _, cause: _ } => CensusBlockNotLocal
    AcquisitionDerivedRosterRefused { module: _, refusal: _ } => CensusBlockNotLocal
  }
}

fn census_block_with_requester(block: CensusBlock, request: AcquisitionRequest) -> CensusBlock {
  match block {
    CensusMemberBlock { module: m, refusal: r, content: c, host_directory: h, requesters: rs } =>
      CensusMemberBlock { module: m, refusal: r, content: c, host_directory: h, requesters: Cons { head: request, tail: rs } }
    CensusEdgeBlock { refusal: r } => CensusEdgeBlock { refusal: r }
  }
}

fn census_blocks_with_requester(blocks: List<CensusBlock>, at: Int, request: AcquisitionRequest) -> List<CensusBlock> {
  match blocks {
    Empty => Empty
    Cons { head: b, tail: rest } =>
      if at == 0 {
        Cons { head: census_block_with_requester(block: b, request: request), tail: rest }
      } else {
        Cons { head: b, tail: census_blocks_with_requester(blocks: rest, at: at - 1, request: request) }
      }
  }
}

fn census_with_state(acc: CensusAcquisitionState, state: AcquisitionState) -> CensusAcquisitionState {
  CensusAcquisitionState { state: state, blocked_members: acc.blocked_members, blocks: acc.blocks, block_count: acc.block_count }
}

fn census_with_edge_block(acc: CensusAcquisitionState, state: AcquisitionState, refusal: CensusEdgeRefusal) -> CensusAcquisitionState {
  CensusAcquisitionState {
    state: state,
    blocked_members: acc.blocked_members,
    blocks: Cons { head: CensusEdgeBlock { refusal: refusal }, tail: acc.blocks },
    block_count: acc.block_count + 1
  }
}

// The member is marked known so a later request reaches it through the lookup's known shortcut --
// AFTER the lookup's cross-tree check, which therefore still denies a requester whose tree may not
// import it (R2) -- and is attached to this block instead of being read again.
fn census_with_member_block(
  acc: CensusAcquisitionState,
  state: AcquisitionState,
  module: String,
  refusal: CensusMemberRefusal,
  content: String,
  host_directory: String,
  request: AcquisitionRequest
) -> CensusAcquisitionState {
  CensusAcquisitionState {
    state: AcquisitionState {
      pending: state.pending,
      modules: state.modules,
      known: map_insert(m: state.known, key: module, value: true),
      absent: state.absent,
      absent_probes: state.absent_probes,
      reads: state.reads,
      held: state.held
    },
    blocked_members: map_insert(m: acc.blocked_members, key: module, value: acc.block_count),
    blocks: Cons {
      head: CensusMemberBlock { module: module, refusal: refusal, content: content, host_directory: host_directory, requesters: [request] },
      tail: acc.blocks
    },
    block_count: acc.block_count + 1
  }
}

// A block in arrival order: its requesters were kept newest first.
fn census_block_in_order(block: CensusBlock) -> CensusBlock {
  match block {
    CensusMemberBlock { module: m, refusal: r, content: c, host_directory: h, requesters: rs } =>
      CensusMemberBlock { module: m, refusal: r, content: c, host_directory: h, requesters: list_reverse(xs: rs) }
    CensusEdgeBlock { refusal: r } => CensusEdgeBlock { refusal: r }
  }
}

fn census_acquisition_finished(acc: CensusAcquisitionState, entries: List<String>) -> CensusAcquisition {
  match list_map(xs: list_reverse(xs: acc.blocks), f: fn(b) { census_block_in_order(block: b) }) {
    Empty =>
      CensusAcquisitionClosed { entries: entries, modules: list_reverse(xs: acc.state.modules), reads: list_reverse(xs: acc.state.reads) }
    Cons { head: first, tail: rest } =>
      CensusAcquisitionBlocked {
        entries: entries,
        modules: list_reverse(xs: acc.state.modules),
        blocked_first: first,
        blocked_rest: rest,
        reads: list_reverse(xs: acc.state.reads)
      }
  }
}

// ONE STEP OF THE CENSUS LOOP: the compiler's lookup, then either the attachment of a requester to a
// member already blocked, or the compiler's verdict step with its refusal recorded.
type CensusStep
  = CensusStepContinued { acc: CensusAcquisitionState }
  | CensusStepEnded { refusal: AcquisitionRefusal }

fn census_verdict_step(env: AcquisitionEnv, acc: CensusAcquisitionState, request: AcquisitionRequest, looked: CandidateLookup) -> CensusStep {
  match acquisition_verdict_step(env: env, request: request, looked: looked) {
    AcquisitionAdvanced { state: next } => CensusStepContinued { acc: census_with_state(acc: acc, state: next) }
    AcquisitionStepRefused { refusal: refusal, state: stopped } =>
      match census_block_locality(refusal: refusal) {
        CensusBlockNotLocal => CensusStepEnded { refusal: refusal }
        CensusBlockedEdge { refusal: edge_refusal } => CensusStepContinued { acc: census_with_edge_block(acc: acc, state: stopped, refusal: edge_refusal) }
        CensusBlockedMember { module: m, refusal: member_refusal } =>
          match looked.verdict {
            CandidateFresh { module: _, file: _, host_directory: directory, content: content } =>
              CensusStepContinued { acc: census_with_member_block(acc: acc, state: stopped, module: m, refusal: member_refusal, content: content, host_directory: directory, request: request) }
            // A member refusal is only produced by admitting a fresh candidate; any other verdict
            // here is a step this loop does not understand, and it ends the run rather than
            // recording a block without the read it came from.
            _ => CensusStepEnded { refusal: refusal }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn census_acquisition_step(env: AcquisitionEnv, acc: CensusAcquisitionState, request: AcquisitionRequest) -> CensusStep {
  let looked = acquisition_lookup(env: env, state: acc.state, request: request, candidates: acquisition_candidates(request: request))
  match looked.verdict {
    CandidateKnown { module: m } =>
      match map_lookup(acc.blocked_members, m) {
        Present { value: at } =>
          CensusStepContinued {
            acc: CensusAcquisitionState {
              state: looked.state,
              blocked_members: acc.blocked_members,
              blocks: census_blocks_with_requester(blocks: acc.blocks, at: acc.block_count - 1 - at, request: request),
              block_count: acc.block_count
            }
          }
        Absent => census_verdict_step(env: env, acc: acc, request: request, looked: looked)
      }
    _ => census_verdict_step(env: env, acc: acc, request: request, looked: looked)
  }
}

fn census_acquisition_loop(env: AcquisitionEnv, acc: CensusAcquisitionState, entries: List<String>) -> CensusAcquisition {
  match list_head(xs: acc.state.pending) {
    Absent => census_acquisition_finished(acc: acc, entries: entries)
    Present { value: request } =>
      match census_acquisition_step(env: env, acc: census_with_state(acc: acc, state: acquisition_popped(state: acc.state)), request: request) {
        CensusStepEnded { refusal: refusal } => CensusAcquisitionRefused { refusal: refusal }
        CensusStepContinued { acc: next } => census_acquisition_loop(env: env, acc: next, entries: entries)
      }
  }
}

fn acquire_census_closure_of(layout: SourceStorageLayout, bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>, entries: List<String>) -> CensusAcquisition {
  match acquisition_env(layout: layout, bindings: bindings) {
    AcquisitionEnvUnprepared { diagnostics: d } => CensusAcquisitionRefused { refusal: AcquisitionGrammarUnprepared { diagnostics: d } }
    AcquisitionEnvReady { env: env } =>
      match acquisition_entry_requests(layout: env.layout, pending: entries, acc: []) {
        AcquisitionEntriesRefused { refusal: refusal } => CensusAcquisitionRefused { refusal: refusal }
        AcquisitionEntriesDerived { requests: requests } =>
          census_acquisition_loop(
            env: env,
            acc: CensusAcquisitionState {
              state: AcquisitionState { pending: requests, modules: [], known: empty_map(), absent: empty_map(), absent_probes: 0, reads: [], held: empty_map() },
              blocked_members: empty_map(),
              blocks: [],
              block_count: 0
            },
            entries: entries
          )
      }
  }
}

// ---- the acquired set as the compiler's source ingest ----------------------------------------------

// The host path an acquired member was read from. It is the artifact id, the artifact path and the
// compilation unit of the member's witness, so a consumer that names the member's file (a discovery
// row's entry, a diagnostic locus) names this path and no second spelling of it.
fn acquisition_module_path(module: AcquiredModule) -> String {
  source_root_child_path(directory: module.host_directory, name: module.file.relative_path)
}

fn acquisition_witness(module: AcquiredModule) -> DagSourceReadWitness {
  acquisition_read_witness(file: module.file, host_directory: module.host_directory, content: module.content)
}

// ONE WITNESS SHAPE FOR EVERY READ THE ACQUIRER MADE, admitted or blocked: the path is the host path
// the bytes came from, and the bytes are the ones read, so a census reporting a blocked member reports
// it from acquisition's read and never from a second one.
fn acquisition_read_witness(file: ModuleFileRef, host_directory: String, content: String) -> DagSourceReadWitness {
  let path = source_root_child_path(directory: host_directory, name: file.relative_path)
  DagSourceReadWitness {
    source: Medium { carried: content, fidelity: Lossless },
    artifact: Artifact { kind: SourceFile, id: symbol_intern_lexeme(lexeme: path), file_path: path },
    compilation_unit: symbol_intern_lexeme(lexeme: path),
    source_root: file.tree
  }
}

// `pending` is consumed from the back so the result keeps the acquisition's order with one cons per
// member.
fn acquisition_ingest_go(pending: List<AcquiredModule>, acc: SourceRootIngest) -> SourceRootIngest {
  match list_head(xs: pending) {
    Absent => acc
    Present { value: acquired } =>
      acquisition_ingest_go(pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1), acc: Cons { head: acquisition_witness(module: acquired), tail: acc })
  }
}

// The acquired members as the ingest the later stages read: exactly the modules requested, each
// carrying the bytes the acquirer read.
fn acquisition_ingest(modules: List<AcquiredModule>) -> SourceRootIngest {
  acquisition_ingest_go(pending: list_reverse(xs: modules), acc: Empty)
}

// THE CENSUS INGEST: the admitted members, then every blocked member's own read, each exactly once.
// A blocked member enters the census so its front-end refusal is reported as a row; an edge block
// read nothing and enters nothing -- it is reported from the acquisition, not from the ingest.
fn census_block_witness(block: CensusBlock) -> Optional<DagSourceReadWitness> {
  match block {
    CensusEdgeBlock { refusal: _ } => optional_absent()
    CensusMemberBlock { module: _, refusal: refusal, content: content, host_directory: directory, requesters: _ } =>
      match refusal {
        CensusMemberFrontEndRefused { file: file, diagnostics: _ } =>
          optional_present(value: acquisition_read_witness(file: file, host_directory: directory, content: content))
        CensusMemberSitesUnreadable { file: file } =>
          optional_present(value: acquisition_read_witness(file: file, host_directory: directory, content: content))
        CensusMemberIdentityDisagrees { file: file, declared: _ } =>
          optional_present(value: acquisition_read_witness(file: file, host_directory: directory, content: content))
      }
  }
}

// Built by cons from the back: the blocked reads in block order, then the acquired members consed
// in front of them by acquisition_ingest_go.
fn census_acquisition_ingest(modules: List<AcquiredModule>, blocks: List<CensusBlock>) -> SourceRootIngest {
  acquisition_ingest_go(
    pending: list_reverse(xs: modules),
    acc: fold_list(
      xs: list_reverse(xs: blocks),
      empty: Empty,
      cons: fn(acc, block) {
        match census_block_witness(block: block) {
          Absent => acc
          Present { value: w } => Cons { head: w, tail: acc }
        }
      }
    )
  )
}

// ---- the read set is the requested closure, exactly (R9) ---------------------------------------------
//
// A pure verdict over a completed acquisition's receipt. The requested set is recomputed from the
// entries and from the requests each acquired member recorded, independently of the probes that
// produced the reads, and the two are compared at identity grain: every read names a member of the
// closure, no file is read twice, every member was read, every member is REACHED from the entries along
// the requests, and every exact request (an entry or an import) names a member. A qualified reference
// that names no module is not an exact request; it was never owed a read.
//
// REACHED, NOT NAMED. A member that some other member's request names is requested only if the module
// making that request is itself requested; a union over every member's requests would certify a cycle
// no entry reaches (A requests B and B requests A), a module that requests itself, and a chain whose only
// head is unreachable. The set is therefore a traversal rooted at the entries. A request selects the
// module the lookup would have read for it: an exact request its spelling, a qualified reference the
// LONGEST of its prefixes that is a member (the lookup probes longest-first and reads the first file
// present, so a longer prefix that is a member would have been the one read, and a shorter member prefix
// is justified only by some other request).
type AcquisitionExactness
  = AcquisitionExact
  | AcquisitionReadWithoutMember { key: String }
  | AcquisitionReadTwice { key: String }
  | AcquisitionMemberNotRead { key: String }
  | AcquisitionReadNotRequested { module: String }
  | AcquisitionRequestedNotRead { spelling: String, from_module: String }

fn acquisition_request_selects(request: AcquisitionRequest, members: Map<String, AcquiredModule>) -> Optional<String> {
  if acquisition_request_exact(request: request) {
    optional_present(value: request.spelling)
  } else {
    list_find_first(
      xs: reference_prefixes(reference: request.spelling),
      predicate: fn(name) { map_contains_key(m: members, key: name) }
    )
  }
}

// The traversal's state: every name reached so far, and the reached names whose requests are still to be
// read (a stack, so a push costs the size of the push).
type AcquisitionReach {
  reached: Map<String, Bool>
  pending: List<String>
}

fn acquisition_reach_visit(reach: AcquisitionReach, name: String) -> AcquisitionReach {
  if text_set_has(set: reach.reached, name: name) {
    reach
  } else {
    AcquisitionReach { reached: map_insert(m: reach.reached, key: name, value: true), pending: Cons { head: name, tail: reach.pending } }
  }
}

fn acquisition_reach_requests(members: Map<String, AcquiredModule>, requests: List<AcquisitionRequest>, reach: AcquisitionReach) -> AcquisitionReach {
  fold_list(xs: requests, empty: reach, cons: fn(acc, request) {
    match acquisition_request_selects(request: request, members: members) {
      Present { value: name } => acquisition_reach_visit(reach: acc, name: name)
      Absent => acc
    }
  })
}

fn acquisition_reach_go(members: Map<String, AcquiredModule>, reach: AcquisitionReach) -> Map<String, Bool> {
  match list_head(xs: reach.pending) {
    Absent => reach.reached
    Present { value: name } => {
      let rest = AcquisitionReach { reached: reach.reached, pending: skip(xs: reach.pending, n: 1) }
      match map_lookup(m: members, key: name) {
        Absent => acquisition_reach_go(members: members, reach: rest)
        Present { value: member } =>
          acquisition_reach_go(members: members, reach: acquisition_reach_requests(members: members, requests: member.requests, reach: rest))
      }
    }
  }
}

// Every name the entries reach through the members' requests. A name that is not a member is reached and
// expands to nothing; whether an exact request owed it a read is acquisition_exactness_requests' question.
fn acquisition_reached(entries: List<String>, modules: List<AcquiredModule>) -> Map<String, Bool> {
  acquisition_reach_go(
    members: fold_list(xs: modules, empty: empty_map(), cons: fn(acc, member) { map_insert(m: acc, key: member.name, value: member) }),
    reach: fold_list(xs: entries, empty: AcquisitionReach { reached: empty_map(), pending: [] }, cons: fn(acc, entry) {
      acquisition_reach_visit(reach: acc, name: entry)
    })
  )
}

fn acquisition_first_duplicate(pending: List<String>, seen: Map<String, Bool>) -> Optional<String> {
  match list_head(xs: pending) {
    Absent => optional_absent()
    Present { value: key } =>
      if text_set_has(set: seen, name: key) {
        optional_present(value: key)
      } else {
        acquisition_first_duplicate(pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1), seen: map_insert(m: seen, key: key, value: true))
      }
  }
}

fn acquisition_first_unmet_exact(pending: List<AcquiredModule>, names: Map<String, Bool>) -> Optional<AcquisitionRequest> {
  match list_head(xs: pending) {
    Absent => optional_absent()
    Present { value: acquired } =>
      match list_find_first(xs: acquired.requests, predicate: fn(request) {
        acquisition_request_exact(request: request) && !text_set_has(set: names, name: request.spelling)
      }) {
        Present { value: request } => optional_present(value: request)
        Absent => acquisition_first_unmet_exact(pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1), names: names)
      }
  }
}

fn acquisition_exactness_requests(entries: List<String>, modules: List<AcquiredModule>, names: Map<String, Bool>) -> AcquisitionExactness {
  match list_find_first(xs: entries, predicate: fn(entry) { !text_set_has(set: names, name: entry) }) {
    Present { value: entry } => AcquisitionRequestedNotRead { spelling: entry, from_module: "" }
    Absent =>
      match acquisition_first_unmet_exact(pending: modules, names: names) {
        Present { value: request } => AcquisitionRequestedNotRead { spelling: request.spelling, from_module: request.from_module }
        Absent => AcquisitionExact {}
      }
  }
}

fn acquisition_exactness_named(entries: List<String>, modules: List<AcquiredModule>) -> AcquisitionExactness {
  let reached = acquisition_reached(entries: entries, modules: modules)
  match list_find_first(xs: modules, predicate: fn(member) { !text_set_has(set: reached, name: member.name) }) {
    Present { value: unrequested } => AcquisitionReadNotRequested { module: unrequested.name }
    Absent =>
      acquisition_exactness_requests(entries: entries, modules: modules, names: text_set_of(names: list_map(xs: modules, f: fn(m) { m.name })))
  }
}

// What a member owes the read set: a file member its own file; a derived roster every input its
// derivation depended on, and never its own storage key (a file there refuses).
fn acquisition_member_owed_reads(member: AcquiredModule) -> List<String> {
  match member.origin {
    AcquiredFromFile => [module_file_key(file: member.file)]
    AcquiredDerivedRoster { inputs: inputs } => inputs
  }
}

fn acquisition_first_unread_owed(modules: List<AcquiredModule>, read_set: Map<String, Bool>) -> Optional<String> {
  match list_head(xs: modules) {
    Absent => optional_absent()
    Present { value: member } =>
      match list_find_first(xs: acquisition_member_owed_reads(member: member), predicate: fn(key) { !text_set_has(set: read_set, name: key) }) {
        Present { value: key } => optional_present(value: key)
        Absent => acquisition_first_unread_owed(modules: skip(xs: modules, n: 1), read_set: read_set)
      }
  }
}

fn acquisition_exactness_reads(entries: List<String>, modules: List<AcquiredModule>, reads: List<String>) -> AcquisitionExactness {
  match acquisition_first_unread_owed(modules: modules, read_set: text_set_of(names: reads)) {
    Present { value: unread } => AcquisitionMemberNotRead { key: unread }
    Absent => acquisition_exactness_named(entries: entries, modules: modules)
  }
}

// The keys every member owes, as one set: what a read may name without being a read outside the closure.
fn acquisition_owed_key_set(modules: List<AcquiredModule>) -> Map<String, Bool> {
  fold_list(xs: modules, empty: empty_map(), cons: fn(acc, member) {
    fold_list(xs: acquisition_member_owed_reads(member: member), empty: acc, cons: fn(set, key) { map_insert(m: set, key: key, value: true) })
  })
}

fn acquisition_exactness_unique(entries: List<String>, modules: List<AcquiredModule>, reads: List<String>) -> AcquisitionExactness {
  match acquisition_first_duplicate(pending: reads, seen: empty_map()) {
    Present { value: key } => AcquisitionReadTwice { key: key }
    Absent => acquisition_exactness_reads(entries: entries, modules: modules, reads: reads)
  }
}

fn acquisition_exactness(entries: List<String>, modules: List<AcquiredModule>, reads: List<String>) -> AcquisitionExactness {
  let member_keys = acquisition_owed_key_set(modules: modules)
  match list_find_first(xs: reads, predicate: fn(key) { !text_set_has(set: member_keys, name: key) }) {
    Present { value: key } => AcquisitionReadWithoutMember { key: key }
    Absent => acquisition_exactness_unique(entries: entries, modules: modules, reads: reads)
  }
}

// ---- the acquisition as a verified set, and as text ---------------------------------------------------
//
// A door that consumes an acquisition owes two things beyond the modules: the exactness verdict (R9's
// check that the read set IS the requested closure) and a located statement of why it did not hold.
// Both are decided here, once, so a consumer is handed either a verified set or a refusal and never
// reads `Acquisition` and `AcquisitionExactness` separately -- two consumers that each chose which of
// the two to trust would be the fork this door exists to prevent.
type VerifiedAcquisition
  = AcquisitionVerified { modules: List<AcquiredModule>, reads: List<String>, absent_probes: Int }
  | AcquisitionNotVerified { reason: Symbol, cause: String }

fn acquisition_request_origin_text(request: AcquisitionRequest) -> String {
  if request.from_module == "" { "the entry list" } else { request.from_module }
}

fn acquisition_request_text(request: AcquisitionRequest) -> String {
  concat(request.spelling, " (requested by ", acquisition_request_origin_text(request: request), ")")
}

fn acquisition_lookup_text(lookup: ReferenceLookup) -> String {
  match lookup {
    ReferenceResolved { module: name, member: _, file: _, content: _ } => concat("resolved to ", name)
    ReferenceUnknownModule { reference: reference } => concat("no module is stored for ", reference)
    ReferenceRefused { module: name, file: file, cause: cause } =>
      concat("module ", name, " at ", module_file_key(file: file), " could not be read: ", cause)
    ReferenceModuleLineMissing { module: name, file: file } =>
      concat("the file at ", module_file_key(file: file), " holds no module line, so it is not module ", name)
    ReferenceModuleLineDisagrees { module: name, declared: declared, file: file } =>
      concat("the file at ", module_file_key(file: file), " declares module ", declared, " where the layout stores ", name)
    ReferenceModuleHeaderRefused { module: name, file: file, cause: cause } =>
      concat("the file at ", module_file_key(file: file), " holds no readable module header, so it is not module ", name, ": ", cause)
    ReferenceCrossTreeDenied { module: name, file: file, reason: reason } =>
      concat("module ", name, " at ", module_file_key(file: file), " ", cross_tree_denial_text(reason: reason))
  }
}

// The reason a crossing was refused, said as what it is: there is no tree direction, so a refusal is either
// an inactive root or a namespace with no row (v2.std.cross_tree.import_model cross_tree_import_admitted).
fn cross_tree_denial_text(reason: CrossTreeDenialReason) -> String {
  match reason {
    CrossTreeNamespaceUndeclared =>
      "is across a tree crossing from or to a namespace with no row in the declared layout"
    CrossTreeToTreeNotInRootSet => "lies in a tree that is not an active source root"
    CrossTreeFromTreeNotInRootSet => "is requested from a tree that is not an active source root"
  }
}

fn acquisition_refusal_text(refusal: AcquisitionRefusal) -> String {
  match refusal {
    AcquisitionGrammarUnprepared { diagnostics: _ } => "the dag grammar did not prepare"
    AcquisitionEntryUnderivable { entry: entry, cause: cause } =>
      concat("entry ", entry, " names no storable module: ", cause)
    AcquisitionTreeUnbound { tree: tree, request: request } =>
      concat("no source root is bound to the ", tree, " tree, which holds ", acquisition_request_text(request: request))
    AcquisitionUnknownModule { request: request } =>
      concat("no module is stored for ", acquisition_request_text(request: request))
    AcquisitionLookupRefused { request: request, lookup: lookup } =>
      concat(acquisition_request_text(request: request), ": ", acquisition_lookup_text(lookup: lookup))
    AcquisitionMemberRefused { module: name, file: file, diagnostics: d } =>
      concat("module ", name, " at ", module_file_key(file: file), " did not front-end: ", symbol_lexeme(sym: diagnostics_fatal_reason(d: d)))
    AcquisitionMemberSitesUnreadable { module: name, file: file } =>
      concat("module ", name, " at ", module_file_key(file: file), " lowered but its reference sites are not all readable")
    AcquisitionMemberIdentityDisagrees { module: name, file: file, declared: declared } =>
      concat("module ", name, " at ", module_file_key(file: file), " parses as module ", declared)
    AcquisitionDerivedRosterRefused { module: name, refusal: carried_refusal } =>
      concat("derived roster ", name, ": ", derived_roster_refusal_text(refusal: carried_refusal))
  }
}

fn derived_roster_refusal_text(refusal: DerivedRosterRefusal) -> String {
  match refusal {
    DerivedRosterOccupied { file: file } =>
      concat("a file occupies its storage path ", module_file_key(file: file), "; the roster is derived from its sibling rows, so that file is never consumed and never overridden -- remove it")
    DerivedRosterProbeRefused { file: file, cause: cause } => concat("its storage path ", module_file_key(file: file), " could not be probed: ", cause)
    DerivedRosterUnlisted { directory: directory, cause: cause } => concat("its row directory ", directory, " could not be listed: ", cause)
    DerivedRosterSiblingUnread { file: file, cause: cause } => concat("sibling ", module_file_key(file: file), " could not be read, and the roster is not derived from fewer rows: ", cause)
    DerivedRosterSiblingMisfiled { file: file, held: held } => concat("sibling ", module_file_key(file: file), ": ", held_misfiled_cause(file: file, held: held))
    DerivedRosterMisplacedRow { stem: stem, declared: declared } =>
      concat("sibling ", stem, " declares a row named ", declared, "; a row is declared under its own file stem")
  }
}

fn acquisition_exactness_text(verdict: AcquisitionExactness) -> String {
  match verdict {
    AcquisitionExact => "exact"
    AcquisitionReadWithoutMember { key: key } => concat("file ", key, " was read and is not a member of the closure")
    AcquisitionReadTwice { key: key } => concat("file ", key, " was read twice")
    AcquisitionMemberNotRead { key: key } => concat("closure member ", key, " was never read")
    AcquisitionReadNotRequested { module: name } => concat("module ", name, " was read and no entry reaches it through the requests")
    AcquisitionRequestedNotRead { spelling: spelling, from_module: from } =>
      concat(spelling, " (requested by ", from, ") names a module that was never read")
  }
}

fn acquisition_exactness_holds(verdict: AcquisitionExactness) -> Bool {
  match verdict {
    AcquisitionExact => true
    AcquisitionReadWithoutMember { key: _ } => false
    AcquisitionReadTwice { key: _ } => false
    AcquisitionMemberNotRead { key: _ } => false
    AcquisitionReadNotRequested { module: _ } => false
    AcquisitionRequestedNotRead { spelling: _, from_module: _ } => false
  }
}

fn acquisition_complete_verified(entries: List<String>, modules: List<AcquiredModule>, reads: List<String>, absent_probes: Int) -> VerifiedAcquisition {
  let verdict = acquisition_exactness(entries: entries, modules: modules, reads: reads)
  if acquisition_exactness_holds(verdict: verdict) {
    AcquisitionVerified { modules: modules, reads: reads, absent_probes: absent_probes }
  } else {
    AcquisitionNotVerified { reason: ^acquisition_inexact, cause: acquisition_exactness_text(verdict: verdict) }
  }
}

fn acquisition_verified(acquisition: Acquisition) -> VerifiedAcquisition {
  match acquisition {
    AcquisitionRefused { refusal: refusal, reads: _ } =>
      AcquisitionNotVerified { reason: ^acquisition_refused, cause: acquisition_refusal_text(refusal: refusal) }
    AcquisitionComplete { entries: entries, modules: modules, reads: reads, absent_probes: absent } =>
      acquisition_complete_verified(entries: entries, modules: modules, reads: reads, absent_probes: absent)
  }
}

// THE ONE DOOR A CONSUMER USES: acquire the closure of `entries` and hand back either the verified
// set or the located reason it is not one.
fn acquire_verified(layout: SourceStorageLayout, bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>, entries: List<String>) -> VerifiedAcquisition {
  acquisition_verified(acquisition: acquire_closure_of(layout: layout, bindings: bindings, entries: entries))
}

// ---- enumerating a namespace (a population job's explicit input) ------------------------------------------
//
// Compiling an entry never walks a directory. A job whose input IS a population (a census, the
// population parse, the native lane's candidate modules) is handed the module names the storage rule
// puts under the namespaces the layout declares (enumerate_bound_modules). A name follows its path under
// the rule, so the enumeration is the inverse of module_file for one directory; the acquirer's `module`
// line check then confirms each name when it is requested. The walk below is the one directory observation
// that enumeration is built from; no caller enumerates a single named namespace.

// What one directory walk observed, before a caller decides what an absent directory means.
type NamespaceWalk
  = NamespaceWalkListed { namespace: String, modules: List<String> }
  | NamespaceWalkAbsent { namespace: String, directory: String }
  | NamespaceWalkRefused { namespace: String, cause: String }

fn namespace_module_name(namespace: String, directory: String, path: String) -> String {
  let prefix_length = string_length(s: source_root_child_path(directory: directory, name: ""))
  concat(namespace, ".", replace(s: substring(s: path, start: prefix_length, end: string_length(s: path) - 4), from: "/", to: "."))
}

// The directory, relative to the tree's host directory, that holds a namespace: the relative path of
// a probe module placed directly in it, less the probe's own file name.
fn namespace_relative_directory(probe_relative_path: String) -> String {
  if probe_relative_path == "x.dag" {
    ""
  } else {
    substring(s: probe_relative_path, start: 0, end: string_length(s: probe_relative_path) - 6)
  }
}

// Whether a refused walk is the namespace directory itself not existing, which is the answer "no
// module of this namespace is stored under this tree" and not a listing that failed. Any other
// refusal, including a missing entry beneath the directory, is a failed listing and stays a refusal.
fn namespace_directory_is_the_absent_path(path: String, directory: String, kind: String) -> Bool {
  if path == directory {
    match admit_filesystem_failure_kind(observed: kind) {
      FilesystemFailureKindAdmitted { kind: FilesystemNotFound } => true
      FilesystemFailureKindAdmitted { kind: _ } => false
      FilesystemFailureKindUnrecognized { observed: _ } => false
    }
  } else {
    false
  }
}

fn namespace_walk(namespace: String, directory: String) -> NamespaceWalk {
  match source_root_walk(pending: [SourceRootWalkTask { path: directory, root: true, origin: directory }], paths: [], suffix: ".dag") {
    SourceRootPathsRefused { path: path, kind: kind, cause: cause } =>
      if namespace_directory_is_the_absent_path(path: path, directory: directory, kind: kind) {
        NamespaceWalkAbsent { namespace: namespace, directory: directory }
      } else {
        NamespaceWalkRefused { namespace: namespace, cause: concat(path, ": ", kind, ": ", cause) }
      }
    SourceRootPathsRead { paths: paths } =>
      NamespaceWalkListed {
        namespace: namespace,
        modules: list_map(xs: paths, f: fn(walked) { namespace_module_name(namespace: namespace, directory: directory, path: walked.path) })
      }
  }
}

fn namespace_walk_under(namespace: String, root: String, relative: String) -> NamespaceWalk {
  if relative == "" {
    namespace_walk(namespace: namespace, directory: root)
  } else {
    namespace_walk(namespace: namespace, directory: source_root_child_path(directory: root, name: relative))
  }
}

// THE MODULES OF EVERY BOUND TREE: a population job's input when the population is the repository
// under the bindings it was handed. The namespaces are the layout's declared rows (namespaces that
// directly contain modules); each tree directory is walked once per top-level namespace they name,
// so nothing is enumerated that the layout does not declare and no namespace is listed twice. A
// top-level namespace whose tree is not bound contributes nothing (the caller bound the trees it
// wants), and so does one with no directory under a bound tree: the rows declare the namespaces of
// the whole repository, and a tree holding only some of them is a smaller population, not a failed
// listing. What that cannot hide is the converse: a row that no governed file realizes is refused by
// the layout check (NamespaceRowWithoutModules), and every bound root must itself be a listable
// directory, so an unreadable or missing tree refuses here instead of reading as empty. Any other
// listing that fails refuses the whole enumeration, never a partial population.
//
// A LISTED MODULE WHOSE NAMESPACE HAS NO ROW REFUSES, located at the module. The walk descends a declared
// top-level namespace's whole directory, so a file in an undeclared sub-namespace (dag/test/zzz/m.dag,
// namespace test.zzz) would otherwise enter the population. This route reads by path and never through the
// storage lookup, so the membership judgment the lookup asks before a crossing
// (v2.std.cross_tree.import_model namespace_declared over the layout's namespace_rows, the one read both make) is asked here, of every
// member: a file outside gunbc.source_layout is not compilable on either route (DESIGN section 3).
type BoundModules
  = BoundModulesListed { modules: List<String> }
  | BoundModulesRefused { subject: String, cause: String }

type BoundRootsListing
  = BoundRootsListable
  | BoundRootUnlistable { tree: String, path: String, kind: String, cause: String }

fn bound_roots_listing(pending: List<SourceRootBinding>) -> BoundRootsListing {
  match list_head(xs: pending) {
    Absent => BoundRootsListable {}
    Present { value: binding } =>
      match source_root_directory(path: binding.host_directory) {
        SourceRootDirectoryListed { path: _ } => bound_roots_listing(pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1))
        SourceRootDirectoryRefused { path: path, kind: kind, cause: cause } =>
          BoundRootUnlistable { tree: source_root_ref_label(tree: binding.tree), path: path, kind: kind, cause: cause }
      }
  }
}

fn bound_modules_prepended(listed: List<String>, acc: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  fold_list(xs: list_reverse(xs: listed), empty: acc, cons: fn(rest, name) { Cons { head: name, tail: rest } })
}

fn namespace_top_level(namespace: String) -> String {
  match list_head(xs: split(s: namespace, delimiter: ".")) {
    Absent => namespace
    Present { value: first } => first
  }
}

fn top_level_namespaces_go(pending: List<String>, seen: Map<String, Bool>, acc: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  match list_head(xs: pending) {
    Absent => acc
    Present { value: namespace } => {
      let top = namespace_top_level(namespace: namespace)
      if text_set_has(set: seen, name: top) {
        top_level_namespaces_go(pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1), seen: seen, acc: acc)
      } else {
        top_level_namespaces_go(pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1), seen: map_insert(m: seen, key: top, value: true), acc: list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: top))
      }
    }
  }
}

// The distinct first segments of the declared namespaces, in the order the rows first name them.
fn top_level_namespaces(namespaces: List<String>) -> List<String> {
  top_level_namespaces_go(pending: namespaces, seen: empty_map(), acc: [])
}

// One walk's listing, admitted to the population or refused at its first member whose namespace has no row.
fn bound_modules_admitted(layout: SourceStorageLayout, listed: List<String>) -> BoundModules {
  match list_find_first(xs: listed, predicate: fn(name) { !namespace_declared(rows: layout.namespace_rows, namespace: module_namespace(name: name)) }) {
    Present { value: undeclared } =>
      BoundModulesRefused {
        subject: undeclared,
        cause: concat("namespace ", module_namespace(name: undeclared), " has no row in the declared layout, so its modules are not compilable")
      }
    Absent => BoundModulesListed { modules: listed }
  }
}

fn enumerate_bound_modules_go(
  layout: SourceStorageLayout,
  bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>,
  pending: List<String>,
  acc: List<String>
) -> BoundModules {
  match list_head(xs: pending) {
    Absent => BoundModulesListed { modules: acc }
    Present { value: namespace } =>
      match module_file(layout: layout, name: concat(namespace, ".x")) {
        ModuleNameUnderivable { name: _, cause: cause } => BoundModulesRefused { subject: namespace, cause: cause }
        ModuleFileDerived { file: file } =>
          match acquisition_host_directory(bindings: bindings, tree: file.tree) {
            Absent => enumerate_bound_modules_go(layout: layout, bindings: bindings, pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1), acc: acc)
            Present { value: root } =>
              match namespace_walk_under(namespace: namespace, root: root, relative: namespace_relative_directory(probe_relative_path: file.relative_path)) {
                NamespaceWalkRefused { namespace: refused, cause: cause } => BoundModulesRefused { subject: refused, cause: cause }
                NamespaceWalkAbsent { namespace: _, directory: _ } =>
                  enumerate_bound_modules_go(layout: layout, bindings: bindings, pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1), acc: acc)
                NamespaceWalkListed { namespace: _, modules: listed } =>
                  match bound_modules_admitted(layout: layout, listed: listed) {
                    BoundModulesRefused { subject: subject, cause: cause } => BoundModulesRefused { subject: subject, cause: cause }
                    BoundModulesListed { modules: admitted } =>
                      enumerate_bound_modules_go(
                        layout: layout,
                        bindings: bindings,
                        pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1),
                        acc: bound_modules_prepended(listed: admitted, acc: acc)
                      )
                  }
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

fn enumerate_bound_modules(layout: SourceStorageLayout, bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>) -> BoundModules {
  match bound_roots_listing(pending: bindings) {
    BoundRootUnlistable { tree: tree, path: path, kind: kind, cause: cause } =>
      BoundModulesRefused { subject: concat("tree ", tree), cause: concat(path, ": ", kind, ": ", cause) }
    BoundRootsListable =>
      match enumerate_bound_modules_go(layout: layout, bindings: bindings, pending: top_level_namespaces(namespaces: layout.namespace_rows), acc: []) {
        BoundModulesRefused { subject: subject, cause: cause } => BoundModulesRefused { subject: subject, cause: cause }
        BoundModulesListed { modules: modules } => bound_modules_with_derived(rosters: layout.derived_rosters, modules: modules)
      }
  }
}

// THE DERIVED ROSTERS ARE MEMBERS OF THE POPULATION THEIR ROWS ARE IN. A roster has no file, so the walk
// cannot list it; it is a member exactly when the walk listed a module directly under its row namespace,
// and it follows the listed modules. A listed module AT the roster's name is a file occupying its storage
// path, which refuses the enumeration rather than standing in for the derived module or beside it.
fn module_is_direct_child(namespace: String, module: String) -> Bool {
  let prefix = concat(namespace, ".")
  module.starts_with(prefix: prefix)
    && !string_contains(s: substring(s: module, start: string_length(s: prefix), end: string_length(s: module)), pattern: ".")
}

fn bound_modules_with_derived(rosters: List<StorageDerivedRoster>, modules: List<String>) -> BoundModules {
  let listed = text_set_of(names: modules)
  fold_list(xs: rosters, empty: BoundModulesListed { modules: modules }, cons: fn(acc, roster) {
    match acc {
      BoundModulesRefused { subject: _, cause: _ } => acc
      BoundModulesListed { modules: so_far } => {
        let name = derived_roster_module(roster: roster)
        if text_set_has(set: listed, name: name) {
          BoundModulesRefused {
            subject: name,
            cause: "a file occupies the storage path of this derived roster; the roster is derived from its sibling rows, so that file is never consumed and never overridden -- remove it"
          }
        } else {
          match list_find_first(xs: modules, predicate: fn(m) { module_is_direct_child(namespace: roster.row_namespace, module: m) }) {
            Present { value: _ } => BoundModulesListed { modules: list_snoc_item(xs: so_far, item: name) }
            Absent => acc
          }
        }
      }
    }
  })
}

// ---- reading a named population (a census's explicit input) -----------------------------------------------
//
// A census exists to report what is wrong with each member of a population, so it cannot be driven by
// the acquirer, whose first unparseable member refuses the whole acquisition. Its input is a list of
// module names (enumerate_bound_modules), and this reads exactly those files -- one read per name, by
// the layout -- carrying no requests and no markers because nothing here asked for a closure. An
// unreadable or absent file refuses the population with its name: a census over a population that
// silently lost a member would report a smaller repository.
type PopulationRead
  = PopulationReadComplete { modules: List<AcquiredModule> }
  | PopulationReadRefused { name: String, cause: String }

type PopulationMember
  = PopulationMemberRead { member: AcquiredModule }
  | PopulationMemberRefused { cause: String }

fn population_member_of(name: String, file: ModuleFileRef, host_directory: String) -> PopulationMember {
  match acquisition_probe(host_directory: host_directory, file: file) {
    ModuleFilePresent { file: _, content: content } =>
      PopulationMemberRead {
        member: AcquiredModule { name: name, file: file, host_directory: host_directory, content: content, requests: [], test_markers: [], origin: AcquiredFromFile }
      }
    ModuleFileAbsent { file: _ } => PopulationMemberRefused { cause: concat("the file ", module_file_key(file: file), " is absent") }
    ModuleFileRefused { file: _, cause: cause } => PopulationMemberRefused { cause: cause }
  }
}

fn population_member(layout: SourceStorageLayout, bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>, name: String) -> PopulationMember {
  match module_file(layout: layout, name: name) {
    ModuleNameUnderivable { name: _, cause: cause } => PopulationMemberRefused { cause: cause }
    ModuleFileDerived { file: file } =>
      match acquisition_host_directory(bindings: bindings, tree: file.tree) {
        Absent => PopulationMemberRefused { cause: concat("tree ", source_root_ref_label(tree: file.tree), " is not bound") }
        Present { value: host_directory } =>
          match derived_roster_named(layout: layout, module: name) {
            Present { value: roster } => population_derived_member(roster: roster, name: name, file: file, host_directory: host_directory)
            Absent => population_member_of(name: name, file: file, host_directory: host_directory)
          }
      }
  }
}

// A DERIVED ROSTER AS A POPULATION MEMBER: the one derivation (derived_roster_derive) with nothing held --
// a population job holds no closure to share bytes with. Every refusal refuses the member with the
// derivation's own cause, so a population never carries a roster derived from fewer rows.
fn population_derived_member(roster: StorageDerivedRoster, name: String, file: ModuleFileRef, host_directory: String) -> PopulationMember {
  match derived_roster_derive(roster: roster, file: file, host_directory: host_directory, held: empty_map()) {
    DerivedRosterAbsent => PopulationMemberRefused { cause: concat("derived roster ", name, ": its row directory is absent, so the population holds no such module") }
    DerivedRosterDerivationRefused { refusal: refusal } =>
      PopulationMemberRefused { cause: acquisition_refusal_text(refusal: AcquisitionDerivedRosterRefused { module: name, refusal: refusal }) }
    DerivedRosterDerived { content: content, inputs: inputs, members: _, siblings: _ } =>
      PopulationMemberRead {
        member: AcquiredModule {
          name: name,
          file: file,
          host_directory: host_directory,
          content: content,
          requests: [],
          test_markers: [],
          origin: AcquiredDerivedRoster { inputs: inputs }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn read_population_go(
  layout: SourceStorageLayout,
  bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>,
  pending: List<String>,
  acc: List<AcquiredModule>
) -> PopulationRead {
  match list_head(xs: pending) {
    Absent => PopulationReadComplete { modules: list_reverse(xs: acc) }
    Present { value: name } =>
      match population_member(layout: layout, bindings: bindings, name: name) {
        PopulationMemberRefused { cause: cause } => PopulationReadRefused { name: name, cause: cause }
        PopulationMemberRead { member: member } =>
          read_population_go(layout: layout, bindings: bindings, pending: skip(xs: pending, n: 1), acc: Cons { head: member, tail: acc })
      }
  }
}

fn read_population(layout: SourceStorageLayout, bindings: List<SourceRootBinding>, names: List<String>) -> PopulationRead {
  read_population_go(layout: layout, bindings: bindings, pending: names, acc: [])
}
